o whisper, which was far more impressive than a "lam a -ghost from a time we both remember, loud speech would have been. '"lf I could lay said the woman. you dead at my feet by speakine, one word —" "I don't,". answered Mix, coolly, "tion't be al " Y .• ' a • " fool! Supper," he sat in to the table. " Ah, . You wou d shout it half a dozen tames, he Italian eggs I You de them well." finished mockingly. "I shouldn't if I were you. e " - "I am glad you like them. Do -you stay here This is a respectabl hotel. long, .11r.—" " Respectable! Where you are?" " Not that mate. I have done with it—at all " I asked you here to talk business, not to waste events in these parts." - time in personalities. Come to the point." "I reckon you maize it to hot to keep," said "What is the point—and the business?" she Mies Jinney, *bitterly. ' asked stllenly. "That is niy business. Igo hack to Salcombe o You will understand after I .tell my story. to-morrow. I might stay there for a few days, or After you left me at that .inn I was recovered go off at once. It 'depends upon my humour. All from the swoon by Mrs. Penny. She raged a lot you have to do is to talk as little as you can about when she found that. you . had gone, but not so me." much as I raged on discovering that you had "I am not one-to talk, Captain Max." stolen those papers concerning Tremewan. I "No. You could always keep your own guessed that you had corne here—to Dartbridge, oosinsel—Ellen." and made up my mind to follow and prevent your Miss Jinnev started as though she had bees doing anything. However, I had no money and struck. "Dent, sir. No one calls me by that could obtain none unless I went to London. I name now. lam Miss Jinney to everyone. That got back, it does not matter how. I begged part name--ah, that name—it brines bae:k the days of the way; I got lifts in carts, and so managed when you and I—when we---=L" she stopped and to get to our old lodgings. There I learned that struck herd at her breast, then .fairly