        <p>" What will you have for breakfast?" "Tom will tell you. I'll give my orders in the morning. Don't bother me at present. Good- night—Ellen." He said the name with a cruel laugh, knowing bow the sound of it wounded her. "Just the same," she said, bitterly, and pressing her band to her heart as though she there felt a cruel pain; "only of yourself you think—only of yourself." "Quite right," replied the Captain, lightly, "I always looked after Number One. You are as sentimental as ever, I see, Ellen." "Women are always fools, and men knaves," said Miss Jinney, and flung out of the room, while Max laughed, much amused. When the door was closed he threw himself into a large chair by the fire and began to smoke. The striking of eight o'clock aroused him, and he sauntered over to the window to see what kind of a night it was. "Dark and rainy," muttered the Captain, drawing up the blind with a jerk. All the better. There will be no spy about. I don't want anyone to know she is visiting ipe. There might be some unpleasantness, and that should always be avoided if possible." The sky was stormy with black clouds which the wind was driving across the face of the watery- looking moon. The tide gurgled against the piles upon which the house was built, and Max caught a glimpse of the sullen waters, grey in the faint moonlight. But even as he looked a drench of rain swept from shore to shore, and blotted out everything. The outlook was as black as the pit, cold, cheerless, and extremely depressing. Not- withstanding the light and warmth of the cosy -oom, he shivered as he drew down the blind, and retreated to the fire- "I wish I had not come now," he muttered. "But as lam here its just as well to get what I want. She must give me what I ask—or "be shook his head menac- ingly, and then laughed at his own melodramatis gesture. For this man usually performed his vil- knio-as actions in a well-bred quiet fashion. He again smoked until the olock chimed the balf-hour. Then he threw his cigarette into the fire, and having carefully locked the outer door, opened the one in the corner. This admitted him into a dark passage, but he was sufficiently well acquainted with it to dispense with a light. Without hesitation, he glided forward, and soon found his hands pressing against the rough wood- work of the outer door. Using the same key, he unlocked this and flung it open. Two figures were waiting in the seini-gloom, a man and a woman. he was huddled up under- the well of the inn as .though trying to escape observation. Max took a malicious pleasure in saying her :name out loud, knowing how afraiad she was of -being discovered on this night adventure, not the 2llo,fft reputable for a woman of her years and position. I am glad to see you, Mrs. Bedowen," he said. 'Pray come in." His tone was so mocking that Tom chuckled, and the housekeeper wished fiercely that she had brought a knife. Had that been so, she had little doubt that it would have found its way into the black heart—as she called it—of the man who was known as Cantain Max.</p>
        <p>tically committed a crime." "None," said Mrs. Bedowen, coolly; "you tried to shoot me, and I was lucky enough to push you over. That is my story. Go on with yours." "You know part of it," said Max, making no csamment. "After I found that Tremewan had been enquiring I came down here to stop the business. I heard of you at Saloornbe, and trucked you there, as I did not want to go to see Tremewan until I had settled you. Well, I found you on Bolts Head, and the little episode of the murder took • place. But I survived, you see." "What devil saved you?" "A fisherman. And he was a .very good sort of devil. He took me to his hut, but I was delirious for a long time. Then, as in my weak state I feared to face you, knowing you would stop at nothing.. I got away to Plymouth in a coasting vessel. I had to earn my bread, and I shipped as steward on board a steamer. to South America. I have been there all these years. Sometimes I made money and at other times I lost it. The old game of cards, you know. It was several times in my mind to return here and see 'what you were doing. I was sorry for Tre- mewan. Finally I came—as you see!" "Why did you come?" demanded the woman, her mouth twitching. "Well, in this way. I managed to save a man's life in South America, and he has stuck to me ever since. Tom is his name—the rest of it doesn't matter. I found that he was a, native of these parts, and it was he who told me that you were the respectable housekeeper of Mr. Francis Tremewan. I thought I would come and see you. I had some money, and bought a small yacht, the 'Petrel,' and in her I came to the Mediterranean —to Monte Casio, in fact. I gambled and lost all my money. I have my yacht, and a small sum, but nothing to Speak of. Then I made up my mind to come and ask for a share of your pick- ings." "Oh!" sneered Mrs. Bedowen, turning even paler. "And this is the honourable man, who declined to blackmail Tremewan!" "Nasty word, blackmail," said Max cheerfully; "the game you have been playing can be called by that name, my dear Judith. I admit that in the first instance I should not have acted on the information contained in those papers. If you remember I refused several times to do what you wished. But you took the matter out of my hands, and you have arranged the affair very nicely. Therefore, as it is on my information that you are making your money, I have come to ask for A portion of it." "I shan't give you a penny," said Mrs. .13edowen, clenching her fist. "Now, consider, my dear Judith," bantered the captain, "you are hardly in a position to refuse. All I have to do is to see Tremewan and. speak out. Then you will be sent to gaol." "You also," she snarled. "Well, that's not improbable," he replied. But I am at such low water already that much worse cannot happen to me. Do you remember Bunyan's song, Msg. Bedowen, 'He that is down need fear no fall'? My case exactly. I think you understand the situatica." "You have the upper hand, sure enough. But it is hard that I should have worked all these years for you." Ali! There you hit upon my weakness. I should have had my share long, long ago. Instead of that, with a mistaken notion of honoul I let you take the money. That is all changed now. You had better say at once whether you will accede to rely demands, as it is getting late. What time are the servanis supposed to be. in at The Gray House? Tremewan is thoroughly respect- able now, I understand." The insult struck home as he intended it should, and Mrs. Bedowen's aniple bosom heaved. How- ever, -tie was not going to give hr enemy the advantage by losims her temper, so she replied calmly, "I am due at ten o'clock." "A most proper hour," s:ticl Max, coolly. " am gllO, to hear that the hiw:,eliral is so well Organised. Yffir werl:-., a.;',iieweii, or the work ef that faslcsl de!l y.:'. 7! a ?" " S'az is not my • :I ill?, woman iercely, rising to her feet eah clutched. hands.</p>
        <p>*Don't you provoke me too far, Max, or I'll no be able to hold myself back. What, do you want?"</p>
        <p>"Money." "I guessed as much, and I have brought some with me. What is the sum?"</p>
        <p>"A thousand pounds is what I require," re- plied Max coolly, and Mrs. Bedowen recoiled with the look of a ,devil on her face.</p>
        <p>"I can't give you that. Here," she produced some notes, "you can have two hundred.</p>
        <p>"It won't do. I have hit on a system to break the bank at Monte Carlo, and it will re- quire a thonsund pounds." "Go and get it, then," shrieked Mrs. Bedowen, dashing the notes on the table. "Ask Tre- mewan. '</p>
        <p>"And you will lose your comfortable situation," he sneered. "If it is bad for me it is a thousand times worse for you," she replied with a glare. "You are driving me into a corner." Captain Max did not want to do that. He knew Mrs. I3edowen of old, and saw that he had driven her to the last extremity. If he pushed her further she was quite capable of—in the vulgar phrase—" cutting off her nose to spite her face." She sat down again with a sullen look, and with a shrug he made the best of a bad bargain. Picking up the notes, he counted them carefully, and bestowed them in his pocket. "This will do just now," he said, slapping his pocket, "I hope that in six months you will give we mare."</p>
        <p>Yot if you wait twenty," she replied coolly. can do nothing more. He is turning crusty." "You must have driven him hard," murmured Mar. "Does he love that doll of a wife less than he did?" "No. He is still infatuated with her. Lord knows why. But even for her sake he won't stand much more. I don't care. I'm down in his will for an annuity." "I congratulate you heartily. Trust you to make yourself comfortable. How is the will maiip?"</p>
        <p>_ "I don't know, although I suspect he has left all the property to his wife and remainder to his daughter after her death. I am sorry for the girl. She is in love with Will Hannaford -" "AK! George Hannaford's son?" "Yes. Neither Hannalord nor Tremewan will hear of the match."</p>
        <p>"I can quite understand," said Max, frowning. They hate one another still, do they?" "Yes. Why do they hate one another?" "That is my secret. You don't know every- thing, Judith." "I know quite enough to feather my own nest," she replied sharply. Then she rose to go. "I hope you won't bother me again. Are you leav- ing this place?" "I don't know. I might stop at Saleombe for a time."</p>
        <p>"Well then, you keep away from me, and I'll snake it worth yourwhile—that is while Tremewan is alive. ' It is to both our interests that he should live, and as he is a healthy man I think he is good for many a. long day. I'm comfort- able," said Mrs. Bedowen with emphasis; "I live on the fat of the land, and my future is secure. But I should not be so well off .as I am now even with my annuity. Therefore I do my best to keep Tremewan alive." Max raised his eyebrows. "You talk as though I intende4_ to murder the man," he said; "his life is equ'ally as valuable to me as to you. By the way, what about Dora Tremewan?" "I have told you," replied the housekeeper sullenly. "She is a nice, pretty girl—worth a dozen of her silly mother. Three days ago I offered to be her friend and to forward the mar- riage with Will Ilanrraford, but she would have none of my Judas kisses. The proper instinct," she finished, with a bitter laugh. "How does she get on with her father?" "He neglects her—in fact, he almost dislikes her, and for the most ridiculous reason. When she was born Mrs. Tremewan nearly lost her life. He has never been able to get over his dislike for the child since."</p>
        <p>"Nonsense. But Tremewan was always eccen- tric. Great heaven, how he must love his wife I" "Ha is crazy about her. Lord only knows why. Nine o'clock. I must hurry," said Mrs. Bedowen, wrapping herself up. "Oh, you can easily be in by ten along with the other servants," he mocked. "By the way, how many are there?" "Three. A cook, a housemaid, and a parlour-</p>
        <p>"Four with yourself. A most economical house- hold. I expect Tremewan is saving his money for his widow. "She won't be one for many a long day if I can help it," retorted the woman, tossing her head. "Well, I hope not, for both our sakes. Will you have Tom to escort you up the hill?" "I'll have no one," snapped Mrs. Bedowen, B,nd dashing aside the hand he proffered she ran into the passage. He heard her open the door and pass out, closing it behind her with a bang. Evidently she was in a very bad temper. " Humph!" said Max to himself. "I wonder if she and Tremewan fight?"</p>
        <p>To be Coutiirued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>NIGHT OF THE CRIME.</head>
        <p>THE NIGHT OF THE CRIME.</p>
        <p>FERGUS HUME.</p>
        <p>Author of "The Mystery of a Hansom Cab," " The Pagan's Cup," "The Crime of the Crystal," "Woman—the Sphinx." Pc. Ptc., etc.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER Hi</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>THZ STRANGER.</head>
        <p>THZ STRANGER.</p>
        <p>Miss Jinney was the landlady of The Eight Bells, a .pu:blichause of no very clean reputation. It stood in a side street, not far from the water's edge. In fact, when the tide was high, the waters washed alts foundation, and one could have dropped from the back window into a boat. In the old smuggling days such a means of flight had often been adopted by those who broke the law when hard pressed by meddlesome Preventive Officers. In those stirring times The Eight Bells had been a noted place, and still .possessed capacious cellars, wherein brandy and lace had formely been stored. Now it was shorn of its glories, and eked out a meagre subsistence by selling beer. No one ever stayed at The Eight Bells. But on this night the inn had a guest,—in fact, two guests, if (roan was to be reckoned as well as master. Towards sunset a stranger, followed by another man, had appeared in the bar, requesting to see Miss Jinney. She made her appearance with all speed, wondering who could want to see her at that hour.</p>
        <p>The landlady was not a comely person. She was over six feet, with a rough head of hair and a red face. And her strength was proverbial,—a reputation not to be despised, seeing that she had to deal with sailors and labourers who were apt to become quarrelsome in thier cups. The fre- quenters of The Eight Bells were afraid of the gigantic landlady, and, in spite of its mean ap- pearance, there was not a better conducted house in Dartbnidge. It had been in Miss Jinney's family for over a hundred years, and she regarded it as her ancestral home. The landlady looked hard at the slender man with the oval delicate face who stood before her. She had a vague kind of idea that she had seen him before, bust could not exactly say when or where. He was young, for in this dark hair streaks of grey were apparent, and there were wrinkles round the eyes. Miss Jinney guessed him to be about forty-five, and looked on him ap- provingly. Not handsome herself, She could yet admire good .(looks in others, especially in a man. Besides this stranger was so elegantly dressed and hooked so aristocratic, that she was decidedly taken aback. "Have you not made a mistake, sir?" asked Miss Janney. "Have I?" replied the gentleman. "Isn't this The Eight Bells?" " Oh, it's the Eight Bells right enough and there isn't a better house in Dartbnidge. All the same we don't entertain the likes of you. There's a first-class hotel up the street." "This is good enough, Miss or Mrs. "Miss Jinney," she said with emphasis. "I'm not married thank God, and don't want to be. The stranger lcoked at her with an amused smile, and she stared hard at hien. Behind stood a tall burly man who looked like a sailor, and who kept his eyes on the lace of his master the whole time. Miss Jinney was puzzled by the pair of them. She might have said as much, but the stranger gave her no time. "A pnivaite room, Miss Jinney," he said, "and you can give as supper as soon as you like. Eggs —you can't very well spoil eggs.' "You get away !" cried Miss Janney, violently. "You're (gallery in your cooking, and—" "I am," replied the stranger, gravely. "There, see that the aneal is good. Fry the eggs in butter, if you know how." " fry nothing," "I think you will," said the man, Sharply. "Am Ito be kept talking here all night?" He stepped forward, and laying one hand on the big woman's arm, whispered softly: Miss Jinney recoiled and gasped, and smacked her large hands together. She knew now why his face had seemed familiar. "It's never you, sir?" she Panted, staring. "Yes," the man looked round anxiously. "Don't speak my name. You can call me Max—Captain Max. Now about the supper." "lit will be ready in half-an-hour," replied Miss Jinney, still staring; "a supper, a private MOM "And one bedroom," finished the Captain. "Tom. here, sleeps on the mat before my door. A good enough place for the dog." MiSs Jkmey looked at the big sailor to see how he took this speech. But he merely showed his teeth in a broad smile. She then led Captain Max into a small back TOOM, the windows of which overlooked the water. It was poorly furnished ss was natural, but clean as a new pin. A bright fire made it look cheerful and homelike.</p>
        <p>Caprbaan Max looked round with the air of a man who knew his way about the premises. "You have not altered this?" he asked, pointing to a small narrow door in the far corner."</p>
        <p>Miss Jinney shook her head. "k's the same as it always was, sir," she replied. ".I see you have not forgotten." "I iforget nothing likely to be of use to me," &amp;said the Captain with a gay smile. "Let me see— oh, yes. That door," he pointed again, "was wade by your grandfather to admit quietly those. who came on smu.ggling business. It leads out into a side-alley that goes down to the water's edge. Anyone can come into the house by this troor, without their presence being suspected in tfhe front of the house. Is that all right, Miss Jinney?" "Yes," replied Miss Jinney, with a scared look, "You can see anyone in this room without its being known." "Good 1" said Max throwing down his hat. •" And it so happens, that I am expegting someone. know I can trust you, Miss J: ..rum‘y. She will arrive sometime after eight o'clock."</p>
        <p>"She? It's a woinani" "A woman whose name you need not know. 'Don't be jealous. 'She is by filo means young. I haven't seen her for some years. I daresay she thought I -waS dead," said the Captain, sardoni- Imlay ; "she does not by this time, though." "Who am I to say is stayiug in .my house?" asked the landlady. "Captain Max, of no particular address. I tame toSalcombe in my yacht "The Petrel," and have come over here kyr the night. That is true, isn't it Tom?" The big sailor drew his haaad acmes .his ,mouth</p>
        <p>Dian's Peaking, end nodded. "That's as true as true," said he. -"The crafit's at Salcombe, sure snow Them as,bas eyes can see." "Ti ,n as has tongues should talk iass,"..replied the 'Captain sharptv, and with a ea.roasm which made the big man ivrinoe. "No matter. ,Get the supper, Miss Jinney, and say what you like about me. Wait--the key.of dais inner door?" Miss Jinney took a rusty key from A canner cup- board and gave it to Max in silence. She then slipped out of the ,room with a dexterity sur- prising in so large a woman, while the Captain took a 01712,1 T by the fire. "My boots, Teen?" The man .went on his ;knees, and removed a pair of elegant tan boots from his master's equally ele- gant feet.. .He then opened a small bag he carried, and produced a pair of slippers. These he slipped on his master's feet, and stood waiting ftrrther orders. For a moment .or so Max regarded him no More than he would have noticed a dog. He lighted a cigarette and looked up. Tom still stood with his faithful eyes on .his tyrant's face. "You can get something to eat," said Max in- dolently; "and afterwards listen in the bar to any go,sip you can pick up about the Tremewan lot. At eight o'clock go up .the hill and meet the woman—you know the way. Bring her to the door of the alley at .half-past eight. I will he There to let you in. If you are late you can finil another master. That's all."</p>
        <p>"You wouldn't chuck me, sir," gtowled the =an, .ntsrvously. "If you did not obey 4me in all things I would," retorted Max." Do you think I saved your life and took: you as my servant to be badly looked after? J3al You know better. Yo.g. can go, Tom."</p>
        <p>The big mall lingered a moment as though he hoped to be dismissed with a more kindly speech. But as Max, gazing dreamily into the lire, took no further notice, he 'departed With a sigh. The Captain heard it with an amused .smile. " What a fool the yellow is," be thought; "be submits to be treated like a crog because I saved his life. However. that ;:sic Jlite, look-out. He's a goodl</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>CHAPTER IV.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER IV.</p>
        <p>BLACKMAIL.</p>
        <p>For a moment Mrs. Bedowen hesitated in the alley. She even stepped back a pace, as though bent upon running away. But Max made no movement to hinder her. He stood in the black doorway, and she instinctively knew that she was smiling in the gloom. Tom, with his hands in his pockets, said nothing, but awaited his master's instructions. Max grew impatient. "Come in," said he sharply. "I can't stay shivering here all night." "And if I do not choose to enter?" demanded the woman, defiantly. The captain shrugged his shourders. "You will give me the trouble of climbing up that very long hill. I daresay Tremewan will be pleased to see me."</p>
        <p>"Yom aro e fiend," broke out the housekeeper but he vat her short. •</p>
        <p>"Not ,so loud, please. Think of your own reputation, if not of mine. What would be said in Dartbridg,e were it known that Mrs. —" The woman gave an angry snarl like that of a wild beast, and pushing past her tormentor, ran up the passage and into the room. Max lingered for a moment ordering Tom to return to the bar of the inn. Shortly he seated himself beside the fire and opposite to his .unwilling guest. For a few minutes there was a dead silence. They looked at one another like accomplished fencers, who had taken the buttons off their foils, and ,were about to fight a duel to the death—which was indeed the case.</p>
        <p>- ---• Mrs. Bedowen did not look her comely placid self at the moment. Her fresh colour was gone, 'and her skin was of a dull white, considerably wrinkled. Even her full lips had lost the hue of life, and her face might have been a malc so passionless did it. appear.. Only her eyes were alive to show the intensity of the feelings which moved her; and they blazed with. a consuming hatred against the man who sat mccking her with his quiet smilt. He was perfectly cool, and amused himself by building up the fire. Tongs in hand he piled up little pieces of coal smiling the while, and Mrs. Bedowen bent upon him her fiery gaze as though she would have slain him. • "Well," said Max, yaw , ~.no doubt you are surprised to see me. •uk, five years since you toppled nut.; over," *2l' ittli at Bolts Head. No doubt you thoughMtou had killed me."</p>
        <p>"I wish I had," said Mrs. Bedowen hoarsely. "You tried to shoot me."</p>
        <p>Max smiled.</p>
        <p>"I remember. It is a pity 1</p>
      </div>
