      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>EVENTS OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>EVENTS OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>"A COUNTRY GIRL."</p>
        <p>The widest interest has been excited in Londe* by the action brought by a playwright, Mr. Augustus Moore, against Mr. George Edwardes, the well-known theatrical manager, to recover €damages from him on- the ground that he had :picked his brains by taking the ideas of a play he had submitted, which was founded on "Charles .O'Malley." and constructing A Country Girl from it. The evidence of Mr. Edwardes was very in- teresting, and showed a good deal of the mechanism .of writing musical comedies of the type for which he is fatuous. Mr. Edwardes' name has grown to .be almost a household word in London and the ;provinces by his numerous succes.ful productions, snd he is practically the father of musical comedy, which forms such a feature of our. modern stage. great test of his b.camen is the fact that lor</p>
        <p>MR. AUGUSTUS MOORE.. MR. GEORGE EDWARDES. practically never puts a failure on the stage, though year by year developing the scope of his operations at the Gaiety, Daly's, and other theatres. Amongst his more recent successes are The Geisha, San Toy, and A Con wry Girl at Daly's, Three Little Maids and The Toreador at the Gaiety. Pre- vious to the last-named piece Mr. Edwardes ran a series of different " Girl " pieces, commencing with The Shop Girl, which made such a hit just over a decade ago. Under his management he altered the traditions of the Gaiety from burlesque; but only a few nights ago he staged as an adjunct to The Toreador, a revue which, under the name of The Linkulan, connected the days of Nellie Farren, Fred Leslie and Kate Vaughan will the present, by recalling their songs and dances, so famous in their time. It is one of the most interesting things in London.</p>
        <p>' . ~•,..-''" cy, f ..,. , l , ~ .. \t„,..,,,.....&amp; c • ~ .;;;,...zi.l.-.,--; ....,, p,,.0„ ~.., 1., :'-:••,: ..,, ~ , • -• .- . 4 , r 0 / ~.,,,) t / ,----- , it ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>MR. EDWARD LLOYD'S DEPARTURE.</head>
        <p>MR. EDWARD LLOYD'S DEPARTURE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Edward Lloyd, the famous singer, who tetired in 1900, but reappeared at an Alert Hall concert recently, has given a further proof of his return to public life by starting on a great tour through Australia and America, which is to last eighteen months. His appearance at the Albert Hall was most enthusiastically greeted, and he was found to be in splendid voice, so, tired of idleness and inactivit2, he decided to accept an offer for a big tour. Mr. • Lloyd had settled down quietly at May- field, and would prob Ably have remained in se- clusion but for the sudden death of his wife,which forced him back to public life from sheer lone- liness. Sailing on the China, which he joined at Marseilles., Mr.</p>
        <p>Lloyd is now on his way to Mel- bourne, and ac- cording to his WRO HAS GONE ON AN AUSTRALIAN present plans, in- tends to give sixteen concerts in different parts</p>
        <p>MR. EDWARD 1.1 YD,</p>
        <p>1 7 * •"*E"?'. , ••• • 4 4 j</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>AND A.MIiatICA.N</head>
        <p>AND A.MIiatICA.N</p>
        <p>of Australia and ten in New Zealand. He has taken with him a concert party, and hopes to make a conquest of the Antipodes, which he has never before visited. This, indeed, and the fact that he had heard of the recent great development of a love for music in Australia attracted him to undertake his present tour. From New Zealand he goes on to America, being due in May of next year at Cincinnati, which he has visited three times before. He hopes to be back in England in the summer of 1904 : but it is doubtful at present whether he will again sing in public in England, as he has not expressed himself upon the point. He is a great favourite with British audiences, and the best of good wishes will accompany him on his long tour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>NEWEST FLOATING PALACE.</head>
        <p>THE NEWEST FLOATING PALACE.</p>
        <p>The Norddeutscher Lloyd Company are sending on her trial journey on April 15th next, the Kaiser lVillteles IP, one of the most elaborate passenger Steamers yet constructed. The most striking new features in the ship are the outcome of arrange- tents for increae:l safety. luxury, and speed with- out vibration. Her prinuipal dimension.; are: Length -706 ft. Gin., (cam 721 t.. depth 521 t. Gin. liter registered trn,r s 0.00 tons, and her displacement 26,0(Pe :on-. tlcr c:ipacity, exclusive of the 5,700 tons of co:A for the bunkers and the engines, is 2.).(00 tons. Her draught to load line is 29ft. T' ere is. a donhle bottom running the whole length of the ...hip, and divided into twenty-six compartments, ea ble of holding over half-a-million gallons of wattq.. The hull itself is divided into nineteen water-tight compartments, the engines being further protected by a lougitudi-</p>
        <p>nal bulkhead. There are in all seven decks, the orlop, lower, main, upper, lower and upper promenade decks, and awning deck. The two propellers have a diameter of 221 t. 10in., and at eighty revolutions will propel the ship at a speed of 23 knots. There are four sets of quadruple expansion engines, aggregating 10,000 indicated borse-power, two sets of engines working on each shaft. 'the engines are balanced on the Schlick system. The passenger accommodation includes two imperial suites with dining, drawing, bed and Bath room; eight state apartments with sitting, bed, and bath room; e:ght state cabins with bath room; in all, 290 first-class cabins and 107 second. The first-class saloon is 108 ft. long by 69ft. broad, snd vovides Etting accommodation for 554 passenger, : the second-class saloon will seat 190 passengers. There is also a children's saloon, smoke room, lii,rary, typewriting room, drawing room, and two Vienna eaft;s. There is a post-office on board, and tile mails will be sorted during the passage and landed at the port of arrival ready for immediate distribution. - The ship is also fitted for wireless telegraphy. Perhaps the fact the summer rate for an imperial suite, accommodating two passengers, from Bremen Harbour to New York, or vice'versa, is £4OO for the single trip, is most eloquent of. provided.</p>
