      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>ST. GILES'S, CRIPPLEGATE.</head>
        <p>ST. GILES'S, CRIPPLEGATE.</p>
        <p>A movement is afloat to save the churcn ot nt. .Giles's, Cripplegate, from the hand of the vandal builder, who proposes to hide it once again from public view. For over two centuries it has been hidden behind houses, which have just been pulled down, and now for the first time the modern Londoner can see the beautiful window, turret and bastion. Not only is this new view of this ancient church beautiful in itself, but the building is one of great historic interest, as in the chancel lie the bones of John Milton, the famous poet. It Was thought at one time that they had been taken IA 16131, when the chancel was raised, but now that is authoritatively known to be incorrscl. The Milton relics are not many, and a great number of meople feel that quite as much respect fez his</p>
        <p>memory should be shown as for that of Shakes- peare. Therefore, the idea is to purchase enough of the land in front of the north-east side to preservf the view an-' -tatue -reat poet</p>
        <p>ST. GILES'S, CRIPPLEGATE, LONDON,</p>
        <p>ANOTHER HISTORIC VICTIM TO IMPROVEMENTS. there, and it is thought that the public will come forward with the funds. The house in which he was born was burnt down in the Great Fire, and the house in which he lived, No. 17, Barbican, was pulled down in 1864, so that the links in the chain with London are gradually being lost.' His cottage in Buckinghamshire is, of course, an historic journeying place to many people and still stands in good condition. Another most interesting point in connection with St. Giles's, Cripplegate, is that Oliver Cromwell was married there on August 22nd to Elizabeth 13ourchier. It is certainly a landmark which ought to be preserved, and it is hoped that those actively concerned with the scheme will find their efforts crowned with success.</p>
