      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>EAST Pr:RTHSHIRE'S NEW MEMBER.</head>
        <p>EAST Pr:RTHSHIRE'S NEW MEMBER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Byburn Buchanan, who has just been retnrked unopposed as Liberal member for East Perthshire, was born near Glasgow in 1846, and, proceeding to Balliol College, Oxford, he had a mot distinguished undergraduate career, taking a double first-class in classical mathematical moderations in. 1867, and a first-class in the final school of Literm Humanioree in 1869, besict s gaining the Stanhope prize for an Enn-lish historical essay in 1868. He took h is BAchelor's</p>
        <p>degree in 1870, in the fAlowieg sear was elected</p>
        <p>73. Fellow o JAII</p>
        <p>Souls, and pro-</p>
        <p>ceeded M.A. in due course. He the was called to Bar at the Inner</p>
        <p>Temple in 1873,</p>
        <p>has never</p>
        <p>practised. Mr. Buchanan, who</p>
        <p>is a Deputy-</p>
        <p>Lieutenant f r</p>
        <p>the city_ and</p>
        <p>county_olthecity</p>
        <p>of Edinburgh, married in 1888</p>
        <p>Emily Oetavia, youngest.daugh-</p>
        <p>ter of the fate THE NEW UNOPPOSED MEMBER Mr. Thotnqs S. FOR EAST PERTHSHIRE.</p>
        <p>Bolitho.ot 'i'reng- wainton, Corn- wall. He has had a somewhat chequered electoral experience. At the General Election of April, 1880, he contested Haddingtonshire against Lord Elcho (now the Earl of Wemyss), and was only beating by forty-four votes. In the following April Mr. John McLaren, one of the members for Edingburgh, being raised to the Judicial Bench. in Scotland, Mr. Buchanan was returned un- opposed as his successor, and when, under the Representation of the People Act, the constituency was /split up into four divisions, he was easily returned, in 1885, for the West Division of the city. He declined to follow Mr. Gladstone in his Home Rule policy in 1886, and at the General Election of that year was returned for West Edinburgh as a Unionist; but, "finding salvation" in Home Rule in 1888, he resigned his seat, and was returned again in February as a Gladstonian. At the General Election of 1892 he was defeated by Viscount NVolmer (now the Earl of Selborne), but in December was returned for East Aberdeen- shire as successor to Mr. P. Esslemont, and continued to represent that constituency until the General Election of 1900, when he was somewhat unexpectedly defeated by Mr. Maconochie by seventy- three votes.</p>
