      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>THREE NOTABLE DEATHS.</head>
        <p>THREE NOTABLE DEATHS.</p>
        <p>Three prominent Englishmen have been laid to rest during the week. They are Dr. Gatling, the inventor of the gun that bears his name, Dr. G-. B. Hill, D.C.L., LL.D., and Mr. H. J. Palmer, the famous editor of the "Yorkshire Post." Dr. .Richard Jordan Gatling was in his eighty-fifth year, having been born in Hertford County, Nova Scotia, on September 12th, 1818. As a boy he helped his father to perfect u machine for sowing cotton seed, and another for thinning cotton plants. His first invention was a screw for propelling water craft. Then he invented in turn machines for sowing rice and wheat. The first revolving battery gun was made at Indianapolis in 1862; four years later his gun was adopted bYthe United States Army. Then it spread all over Europe. The Gatling gun discharged 1,200 shots a minute, and was superseded only by the Maxim. Dr. George Birkbeck Hill, D.C.L., LL.D., was the son of Mr. Arthur Hill, and nephew of Sir Rowland Hill, the organiser of the 'Penny Post. He was born at Tottenham in 1835, and was educated at Bruce Castle School, and Pembroke College, Oxford. In 1859 he was appointed headmaster of his old school; and having occupied the position for nearly twenty years he retired in 1876 in order to devote himself to literary work. He</p>
        <p>TUE LATE DR.</p>
        <p>GATLING.</p>
