      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>LATE MR, H. J. PALMER.</head>
        <p>LATE MR, H. J. PALMER.</p>
        <p>LATE DR. G. 13. HILL, D.C.L., LL.D. will be remembered for his contributions to John- sonian records. In 1878 he published his "Dr. Johnson, his Friends and his Critics," and this was 'followed by " Boswell's Correspondence" in 1879. He edited the autobiographical notes of Sir Row- land Hill, a book which deals at some length with the history of postal reform. The fine edition of Boswell's "Life of Johnson," which Dr. Hill edited, was published in 1887. and ten years later he produced " Johnsonian Miscellanies." Among other works edited by Dr. Hill were " Letters of D. G. Rossetti," and "Unpublished Letters of Dean Swift." In recognition of his literary services he was elected an honorary Fellow of Pembroke College, Oxford. Mr. Palmer was born at Wotton, near Gloucester, in December, 1853, and was thus in his fiftieth year. Becoming a clerk in the service of the Midland Railway Company, he earned rapid promotion, but a fasci- nation for journalism and casual contributions to the Press brought him under the notice of the late Sir William Leng, who invited him to join the staff of the "Sheffield Telegraph," which he did as junior sub-editor Six years latec Mr. Palmer became assistant editor. In 1886 he was appointed editor of the "Birmingham Gazette," and did suah notable work in the Midlands in the four years that followed that, on the death of Mr. Charles Pebody, the directors of the "Yorkshire Post" offered him the appointment at Leeds, which he filled to the day of his death. About eighteen months ago Mr. Palmer became president of the Institute of Journalists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>Examiner, Friday, March 6, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Friday, March 6, 1903.</p>
        <p>THE POLICEMAN'S HAND.</p>
        <p>Raised in the midst of crowded traffic, the Police- man's Hand brings order out of chaos, as he diverts directs, and skilfully controls the hurrying horses. The Birmingham "bobby" does this work to perfection, says a local paper describing a mission of its represents-</p>
        <p>Official position for regulatixg traffic. tive. Here, at 5, Tennant-street, Five Ways, Edgbaston, Birmingham, Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people effected one of their extraordinary cures, in the case of Miss Stanton. _ _</p>
        <p>For about a year." said she, "I had suffered greatly from indigestion,which grow worse and worse in spite of the remedies I took. At the Hospital I was told that, owing to my anEemic state and my neglect of the persistent indigestion which acutely troubled me, the disease shad caused gastric ulcer—that is, an actual sore on the inner coat of the stomach. I experienced gnaw- ing pain if I took even the slightest food, aud a sicken- ing depression. When I did manage to take a little it did me no good, for my stomach would not retain it. "I had almost lost hope, when an acquaintance, living at Edgbaston, Birmingham, advised me to try Dr. Williams' pink pills. I bought a box, and before I had finished it I was a great deal better. The pain grew less severe, and I was able to enjoy and retain my food. I continued to take the pills, and grew stronger and better in consequence. I can honestly say that I never felt better than I do at this moment." Neglected indigestion leads to the frightful internal injury of which Miss Stanton was cured by Dr. Williams' pink pills. The wise will not let disease go so far. These pills have promptly cured anwmia, consumption, paralysis, rheumatism, St. Vitus' dance, nervous and spinal weakness, and the ailments which torture silent women. An article especially illustrating their- value to men and in cases of paralysis is printed elsewhere in this week's issue. The simple story of Miss Stanton should direct all readers to the means of cure just as surely and as unmistakably as the Policeman's Hand directs the Traffic whither it must go.</p>
        <p>1 LOCAL FOOTBALL.</p>
