        <p>Salford had a good opportunity to equalize in front of goal hut Lomas made a poor effort to steer the leather over the bar. A dangerous run was neatly foiled by Houghton. Moran and Jolley by hard play set their line in motion Jones having a grand opening. A tricky run and kick by Moran placed the scrum of the visitors line, Walker losing the ball at the critical moment. Smith worked the leather away Houghton being called on to defend. Butterworth changed the scene and for a short time both ends were visited in turn. After desultory play Houghton finding touch at the corneri again gavelthe Linnets a chance, and continued scrummaging on the line was finely broken up by the visitors, Eventually Jolly from the pack secured and dropped a fine goal. The visitors kicked well into touch on the kick off and disaster was but narrowly averted. Jolley got the leather away and a jurious attack made Smith touch down twice in succes- sion. The Linnets were kept on the defensive after this the" Reds" screwing the scrums over and over again. A desperate attempt to score by Norris was stopped by Houghton who held his man on the line,and Butterworth further placed his side out of danger. Exciting play followed between the opposing front ranks, both teams resorting to association methods.— Final, Runcorn two goals, Salford, nil. ST. HELENS v. HALIFAX.: THE BOTTOM CLUB DEFEAT THE LEADERS. The opposition of the League leaders and the "wooden spoonists" at St. Helens occasioned great interest. The start was delayed for fifteen minutes owing to Little and Reilly missing train connections. There was a good attendance of spectators. The visitors won the toss, and Lea started the ball for St. Helens, but Morley and Riley transferred play to the home quarters. Liversage pain a splendid run, and a strong rush by the home forwards was spoiled by Little kick- ing dead. Keeping up the pressure the Saints finally overcame the Halifax defence, and Lea gained a try, though the score was not improved upon. Liversage was again cheered for another brillant run, and Carney just failed to score. Morley relieved the pressure, and Williams, intercepting a pass, ran well down the field, and transferred to Riley, who scored for Halifax. Little failed at goal, the ball striking an upright. Half-time came with the scores 3 points each. On ends being changed St. Helens had the windin their favour, and quickly put on pressure. The Halifax forwards tried to break away, but the Saints, playing splendidly, kept them back. Hall, from a mark, kicked a splendid goal, and gave St. Helens a 2 points' lead. The visitors seemed completely, over-played, and the result was put beyond doubt when Hall landed another goal for the home team. Towards the close Halifax put forth every effort, but the home defenders drove them back, and St. Helens gained a really clever victory. Result :—St. Helens 2 goals I try (7 points), Halifax I try (3 points). Teams : St. Helens.—Foulkes, back; Liversage, Wearing, Barnes, and Creevey, three-quarter backs; Hall and Banks, half backs; Carney, Lea, Briers, Whiteley, Thompson, Mavity, Mawson, and Green, forwards. Halifax.—Little, back ; Wedgwood, Reilly, Williams, and Rigg, three-quarter backs; Littleton and Morley, half backs • Kitson, Riley, Mallinson, Bartle, Lang- holm, Winskill, Bulmer, and Norbury, forwards. Referee, Mr. J. Oakland, Wakefield. HULL KINGSTON ROVERS v. WIDNES. This match, which was played on the Rovers ground in Craven-street, proved uninteresting. The home team won the toss, and the encounter was a poor-spirited one until Read got away and passed to Morfitt, who threw to West. It was nicely placed, and Starks taking the ball at top speed, got over near the posts, and Kemp added the goal. Widnes had a chance from a mark, but Tilly missed it, although it was right in front of the posts. Five points to nil was the first-half score, amid it remained unaltered.</p>
        <p>The only incident worth recording in the second half was when Morfitt, taking the ball from Starks, appeared to have an open course, when he was overturned at a critical point, and Bell, Read, and West were all tackled on the line in rapid succession. After this came a penalty kick, and relief to the visitors. Result :—Hull Kingston Rovers 1 goal 1 try (5 points), Widnes nil. Teams :</p>
        <p>Hull Kingston Rovers—Sinclair, back; West, Morfitt. Phipps, and Bell, three-quarter backs; Carde and Read, half backs; Starks, Kemp, Feetham, Ellis, Windle, Townsley, Pickering, and Golding, forwards. Widnes—Tilley, back; Peacock, Brown, Rispan, and Hughes three-quarter backs ; Laney and Douglas, half backs; Hughes, Leyshon, Lynch, Rigby, McDonald, Riley, Power, and Webster, forwards. Referee, Mr. J. Bruckshaw (Stockport). Touch judges, Messrs. H. Tomes (York) and W. Jackson (Hunslet).</p>
        <p>BROUGHTON RANGERS v. BRADFORD. Played at Broughton, in wet weather, before 5000 people. Laidlaw started against the wind for the visitors, and Broughton pressed. Sam James from a mark kicked a goal. In the second half the home side still had the best of matter, and Kirkham missed a splendid opening. Declaaa made a good effort for Bradford. Result—Broughton, one goal (2 points) ; Bradford, nil. BRIGHOUSE RANGERS v. LEIGH. At Brighouse, before 3000 spectators. Leigh played against the wind and slope, and were mainly on the defence, but Brighouse lacked scoring power. Nothing was scored at the interval. In the second half Mollineaux dropped a goal for Leigh,and Ganleygscored, Johnson improving. Rasult—Leigh, 2 goals, one try (7 points) ; Brighouse, nil.l</p>
        <p>HUNSLET Y. WIGAN. Played at Hamlet, before 5,000 people. In the first half Albert Goldthorpe dropped a goal, and his brother Walter kicked one, from a mark from nearly half way. After the interval Albert Goldthorpe repeated his feat of dropping a goal. H. Whiteley added a try, A. Gold- thorpe goa ing, and Hunslet won by 11 points to nil.</p>
        <p>BATLEY v. SWINTON. Five thousand spectators witnessed an interesting game at Mount-pleasant, Batley. The home side were in grand form, and before the interval Fitzgerald placed a try and Davies two goals to the Yorkshiremen's credit. Swinton were repeatedly in difficulties in the second half. The Swinton forwards rushed the ball over the line, and scored. Evans kicked two goals. Re- sult—Batley, 7 points; Swinton, 7 points.</p>
