      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>OURLL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OURLL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>St. Peter's Church.</p>
        <p>On Sunday the annual sermons in connection with the Church Missionary Society were preached by the Rev. C. F. Jones, of Liverpool, there being good congregations. In the afternoon a men's service was held, when prizes for regular attendance were distributed to about 60 or 70 men by the Rev. D. R. James, curate, who gave an appropriate address. Mr. T. S. Steel also spoke, giving the members valuable advice as to the best books to read to be of use to them in after life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>B aptist Church, Colborne-street.</head>
        <p>B aptist Church, Colborne-street.</p>
        <p>The subject of the sermon on Sunday evening last at the above church, was "The Atonement." After dealing with objections received in connection with previous addresses, the Pastor stated that while there have been many theories of the atonement, underneath them all isthe unchanging fact that through the death of Christ forgiveness has been given to men, and that it is the fact, and ilot the theory, of the atonement that saves. Mr. Dunnico further stated that there were two great theories on this subject at the present day, viz., the Unitarian'aspect, that Jesus Christ redeems us by revealing God's love for us, and the Evangelical aspect that Jesus Christ reveals God's love for us by redeeming us. These aspects were explained and it was shown that while man cannot atone for his own sin, it can be ce.ncelled through the redemptives work of Christ.</p>
        <p>Councillor Upson and Greatness. Councillor Upson was the speaker at a largely attended open meeting of the P.S.A., which was held in the Methodist Free Church, Buckley-street, on Sunday. The chair was taken by Captain W. Clark, of Liverpool. Mr. S. Hassan gave two tenor solos in a very effective manner. Mr. A. Bates contributed two violin solos (a) "0 star of Eve"; (b) "Melody in G"; and (a) "Graumerei," (b) "Schlummerlied," which were much appreciated. The accompanist was Mr. Albert E. Trow. Councillor Upson, in the course of his address, spoke upon two kinds of great- ness. The first greatness was that which was obtained by a great cost to others; take the case of a warrior. •While one man got thehonour and praise,they must remember it was at the cost of life, of property, of widows' tears, and despairing cries. The other great- ness MIS that of serving others. If one would look to the great life of the Master, he would find that the greatness which Chrfst obtained was that by serving those who were in bondage, in weakness, in sickness, in sorrow. Some of them might be tempted to pass by those of the slums who were poor and wretched, with- out giving them any help ; yea, even treating them with contempt. But, though their image might be defaced, and the coin might be bad, still they were made in the image of their Maker. If they wished to be great, they would obtain that greatness best by serving their fellow men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
        <p>FLETCHER RUSSELS V. SERGEANTS (IST. S.L.P.W.V.B. A most enjoyable game was played on Wednesday last at the Drill Hall, Warrington, and resulted in a victory for the visitors by 38. For the winners Hughes, H. Berry) and Litton played exceptionally well and helped to reverse the result of the previous game played between these teams. FLETCHER RUSSELL. SERGEANTS. T. Bridge 62 Sergt.-Inst. Connor ... 75 F. Berry H. Berry J. W. Evans 75 Sergt. T. Lee 6B J. Smith F. Hughes 75 Sergt. J. W. PlinstoL 43 T. Litton</p>
        <p>Majority 38.</p>
        <p>THE LEAGUE.---FIRST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. HULL.</p>
        <p>The return match with Hull was played on the War- rington ground on Saturday before 3000 spectators. Just before the time for kicking off raid commenced to fall and this doubtless affected the attendance. Both sides were well represented the only absentee being Fish from the Warrington ranks. The ground was in fair order, and the teams and officials turned out as under: Warrington.—Hallam, back; Hockenhall, Isherwood, Dickemson,and Harris, three-quarter backs; Edmonds and Duckworth, half backs ; Jolley, J. Swift, F. Swift, Lunt, Edmondson, Forster,Naylor,and Ashton forwards.</p>
        <p>Hull.—Taylor, back; Jackson, Cook, Morgan, and Parry, three-quarter backs; Franks and Goddard, hal backs; Wade, Rhodes, Harrison, Fulton, Lewis, Eller- ington, Miller, and Parkinson, forwards. Referee Mr. Renton, Hunslet. Touch judges, Messrs. W. Jones, Runcorn, and E. Williams, Widnes. Harrison kicked off for Hull against the wind and Dickenson returned. A fine breakaway by Parry almost ended in Hull getting to the Warrington line, but Hallam saved the risk, and a poor kick by Jackson gave the Warrington forwards a chance, Taylor being very smart in clearing. Hockenhall and Edmonds got going, but Cooke marked, and again prevented Warrington getting in. Some amusement was caused when Franks got clear away, and was then tackled by a forward on his own side. Dickenson madehis mark, and kicked for goal, the ball hitting the goalposts and rebounding into the field of play. Harris made his mark the next minute, and again Dickenson almost kicked a goal. Still the play continued in the Hull half of the ground, and Taylor was very lucky in saving on the line. After Jackson had badly mulled, good passing between Edmonds, Duckworth, Dickenson, Isherwood, and Hockenhall, gained considerable ground, but the next minute Isherwood, getting the ball, and being in difficulties, kicked across, Dickenson snap- ping up the ball on the line end scoring close to the post. Ishenvood kicked a goal. Rain came down in torrents at this stage of thi3 game and caused much discomfort, but Hull forged ahead,and in the teeth of it Parry, after a splendid round of pass- ing, scored at the corner, but no goal resulted. War rington now had a turn, and Dickenson scored a goal from a penalty. Warrington still pressed, and a forward dribble was stopped luckily by a flying kick by Taylor Even play followed, and the sodden state of the ground greatly interfered with the Movement of the players. A score seemed imminent when Duckworth kicked across to Harris and the latter was going full speed for the line when Taylor knocked him in touch. Jackson put a different aspect on the game when he got possession and sprinted down the field. Luckily for Warrington Hallam floored him a few feet from the line, and the forwards by a strong run took the ball to the centre. Morgan gave Warrington a fright by taking the ball at full speed, but Hockenhall and Isherwood succeeded in flooring him. A pod chance of scoring by Warrington was spoiled when Hockenhall failed to hold the heavy ball after a sharp round of passing. The hardest of lines ' eame to Harris, who dribbled half the length of the field, and then, when he had passed everyone, had the bad luck to see the ball roll dead just before reaching it. Half- time came a moment later, Warrington leading by 7 points to 3 points. Swift restarted, and no return was made. Duckworth. broke away, but his pass to Hockenhall sent the ball in touch. Hull, by tall kicking, got a foot in Warrington territory, but a sensational breakaway by Edmonds and a pass to Dickenson ought to have ended in a score,but Dickenson's pass to Forster was forward. This, how- ever, put Warrington in a favourable position, and Hull had some near shaves. Hull, however, again got to the other end, and Goddard was only stopped when close to the line, Duckworth doing the trick. Naylor headed a dribble to the centre, and the home forwards looked like getting a try, for they had beaten Taylor, when W. Ashton got offside and the whistle immediately sounded. Hull soon attacked again with the aid of the wind but the backs were never allowed much latitude. Franks nearly dodged through himself, but was grassed by Forster, who had come out of the pack owing to Edmonds being injured. Though a man short Warring- ton stood up well. Fulton next received marching orders for foully charging Dickenson, and the teams were again evenly balanced. Warrington attacked to the cloae, Dickenson failing at a penalty kick. Final Warrington 2 ;goals 1 'try (7 points ) ; Hull 1 try (3 point).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>RUNCORN v. SALFORD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. SALFORD.</p>
        <p>The above teams in-Let..On't:hWir—w-e—li--I—ane enclosure on Saturday. Considerable interest centred in the match, there being a splendid attendance when both teams flied on the field. The day was cloudy, the ground being a trifle heavy, and the game was spoiled by slight showers. Both played representative teams, Walker taking the place of Richardson. Runcorn lost the toss, Messer replying and Moran letting the visitors in. A free kick relieved, Jolley finding touch in the "Reds' "quarters after a brilliant forward rush. Four penalties in succes- sion for feet up neutralized the advantage. A little later the Salford backs got to work in grand style, and but for a magnificent defence a score would have been registered. Exciting play followed, both teams getting away gallantly. Salford still maintained the upper hand, a strong wind and a blinding rain giving them a decided advantage. The Linnets, however, vigorously repelled the determined attacks, Jones and Butterworth gained considerable ground with smart collaring. A kickacross by Jolley gave Moran a chance, but the defence was keen. Smith relieved, Houghton being collared before he could part. Runcorn still kept up the pressure, a grand opportunity being lost by Moran mulling. The visitors after this got away Brown marking and a shot at goal failing. A forward dribble was well saved by Salford, Bell finding touch in the home 25. Jones, Walker and Butterworth with well judged dribbling and tackling enabled Langley to cross the line but the ball was rulefl back. Houghton again placed the visitors on the defence, Salford afterwards being penalized in front of their own goals. The kick was far out but Farmer rose to the occasion right nobly and landed a capital goal. Walker and Butterworth were again quickly to the fore a neat dribbling giving the home team the advantage. The position was further improved by a run and dribble fromMoran,and Jolly with a couple of judicious kicks got into the visitors corner and a score seemed imminent. Feet up, again averted the danger and up to half-time both teams pressed alternately. Half-time: Runcorn, one goal, Salford nil. The visitors opened the second half with a weak attack which momentarily gave them the advantage. Judicious tackling averted the danger and " It should be remembered also that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>MAZAWATTEE COCOA</head>
        <p>MAZAWATTEE COCOA</p>
        <p>is not only pure but very strong, so you will not require so much of it as of ordinary Cocoas. Hitherto we have had few, if any, such good Cocoas as MAZAWATTE&amp; COCOA on our breakfast table."</p>
        <p>GORDON iftißLEe, M.D., B.A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>WARRINGTON CRICKET AND BOWLING CLUB. A Successful Season.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CRICKET AND BOWLING CLUB. A Successful Season.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. H. SII,COCK (President), presidedat the annual meeting of the Warrington Cricket and Bowling Club which was held at the Patten Arms Hotel on Thursday last week. There was algood attendance.</p>
        <p>A SUCCESSFUL YEAR.</p>
        <p>in the absence tinTeiiii-a—iiidisi;;;liion, of the hon. treasurer (Mr. W. H. Robinson), Councillor Tinnion submitted the balance sheet. He said he felt it was his duty to congratulate the president on the fact that during his year of office the club had been exceedingly successful. It was very gratifying to him as auditor to think that considering every- thing they had come out so very satisfactorily He thought the work of the committee during the year had given satisfaction to everybody, and the condition of the ground and club property was to his mind a very great improvement on what they had had in the past. Al- though they commenced the year with a balance in hand of £l4 4s. 2d. and finished with a debit balance of £6O 125.11 d. bile year had been a very successful one. There was an extraordinary expenditure on the repair of the pavilion and other items of £lB3 ss. Special donations amounting to £6O is. 8d were received, leaving an extra expenditure of £123 3s. 4d. He considered that the financial position reflected infinite credit upon the management of the club.</p>
        <p>ThiceiraißmAN moved the adoption of the balance sheet. Mr. DEWHITRST seconded and said the club had had one of the most successful years ever experienced since the club was formed.</p>
        <p>The motion was agreed to. ELECTION OF PRESIDENT. 31. R. SILCOCK A-ND THE SEASON'S WORK.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said he could look back the season as a very pleasant one to him, and he was now able to say in the common parlance of directors of companies, Ste. that the condition and prospects of the club continued in every way satisfactory. Mr. Sil- cock referred to the expenditure of £llO on the thorough overhauling of the pavilion and said Messrs. William and Segar Owen were entitled to the thanks of the Club for making the preliminary examination free of charge. The committee thought it would be a pity to have the cost of £llO standing as an incubus to the Club for years to come and an effort was made to clear it off by means of voluntary subscriptions. The amount raised in this way was LGO. When it was considered that upwards of £2.0 was subscribed by the committee and honorary members he did not think itspokewell for the playing members. It was a disappointment that a balance had to be carried forward to the next account. Mr. Silcock went on to thank the members of the elevens for keeping down the expenses attached to the matches. He thought that in the playing members the club had a very valuable asset. The past season had been unusually successful, inasmuch as more matches had been won than ever before. Had the weather been favourable the gate money would have gone up by leaps and bounds. Given fine weather during the forthcoming season, they might reasonably anticipate better matches, better gates, and a more successful season all round. He thanked all the members for the kindness, courtesy, and consideration they had shown towards him during his year of office. He had now very great pleasure in proposing the election to the office of President of a gentleman who had been associated with the club for many years and had always taken the deepest interest in its welfare. Although he had not often been aplaying member lately, the members of the club always felt they had a very good friend in Mr. Henry Greenall. (Applause). Mr. W. RICHARDSON seconded, and the motion was heartily agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>MR. SILCOCK THANKED.</head>
        <p>MR. SILCOCK THANKED.</p>
        <p>Councillor PIERPOINT said he was very glad to find that the old club of Warrington was progressing in a manner that was satisfactory to everyone concerned. He was sure they would agree with him that the club was very much indebted to the late President. For many years his efforts had tended in the direction of the successful operations of the club. He (Councillor Pierpoint, did not, however assimilate some of MR. SmcocK's remarks in referrence to the cost of the repairs to the pavilion not having been fully subscribed to by some of the young members of the club. He was sure MR. SH.cocx meant it all in good part but he would like to remind him that some of the members of the team had got an excellent fund of animal spirits, they had good constitutions, and were doing their very best to play cricket, but their resources were not of such a character as would enable them to afford very much more than the usual expenses attendant upon playing. He proposed a Learty vote of thanks to the retiring President and also moved that his name be added to the list of Vice-presidents. He expressed regret that MR. SILCOCR had not seen his way to accept office for another year. Mr. DEwnunsTseconded, and Mr. C. H, UNSWORTH (secretary) supported. The motion was heartily agreed to, and the CHAIR- MAN returned thanks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>TREASURERSHIP,</head>
        <p>THE TREASURERSHIP,</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN expressed regret at the cause of Mr. Robinson's absence, and proposed his re-election to the office of treasurer. He had carried out the duties in a manmer which could not be excelled, and probably not equalled by anybody. Councillor TIN'NION seconded the proposition, which was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>RE-ELECTION' OF THE SECRETARY.</p>
        <p>Councillor TECNION proposed the re-election of Mr. C. H. Unsworth as hon. secretary. If the chairman deserved so much praise and credit for a year's work, what did the secretary deserve for the services of a life- time? The club was very fortunate in having such a gentleman as secretary, Mr. Unsworth always being anxious to do everything he possibly could to further the interests of the club. Mr. W. RICHARDSON seconded and said Mr. Unsworth had conducted the business with great judgment and tact.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>CAPTAIN AND CAUSES OF SUCCESS.</head>
        <p>THE CAPTAIN AND CAUSES OF SUCCESS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. W. BIBBY was unanimously re-elected Captain of the first eleven, and in returning thanks said the past season was the best they had experienced for some years. He thought they would have to go many seasons back to find one in whichthey were only defeated twice. He put the success down to three reasons, the first being that they were able to play it regular team, the second that all the members practised regularly, and the third the excellent assistance they received from their pro- fessional, particularly at the nets. He thanked Mr. Callon (sub-captain), the members of the first eleven and also Mr. Edward Pi erpo int (captain of the second eleven) for their willing assistance. The members of the second eleven whom he had to call upon to assist occasionally had also given very willing and loyal support and he thanked them for it. If the members worked as they had done during the past season he did not see any reason why this season should not be as successful.</p>
        <p>HUDDERSFIELD V. OLDHAM. At Halifax, before 6,000 spectators. Oldham kicked off, and play remained veryeven, neither side being able to score. fluddersfield restarted, and attacked, Dew- hurst scoring in the corner. The game was keenly con- tested towards the close. Huddersfield scoring an uncon- verted try. Result—Huddersfield, 2 tries (6 points) ; Oldham, nil. RUNCORN v. BROUGHTON RANGERS. The above match was played at Runcorn on Wednes- day before a moderate crowd, under good climatic con- ditions. Runcorn won the toss and elected to play with a slight breeze. The visitors at once were placed on the defensive, a neat round of passing being initiated by Richardson. A scrum was formed between the posts, and, within a minute from the start, Jolley obtained the ball and landed a fine goal. After this reverse the visi- tors got away, a grand run by Wilson and Hogg nearly piercing the home defence. A penalty relieved, Field- ing kicking poorly into touch. Immediately after Field- ing was collared with the ball, but no score resulted from a good chance. Jones was afterwards conspicuous in collaring his man and the home backs again got go- ing, smart play by Price, Moran, and Richardson making considerable headway. S. Jones got the leather away, Fielding having a furtile drop at goal . A brilliant attempt to break away by Hogg failed. The 'visitors attacked. a piece of sheer luck saving Runcorn. Judici- ous kicking by Houghton made the play in the visitor's 25 and after an exciting bit Jolley kicked across to Langley who dropped on the leather. Farmer added the necessary points. As soon as the ball was set in motion price and Moran nearly effected a score. The ball was sent back, Jolley and Houghton kicking well into touch. Feet up relieved. The game continued to Warrington County Petty Sessions. be very evenly contested both teams attacking and de- fending. Hogg's career was neatly stopped by Price WEDNESDAY --Before Colonel Ridgway (in the chair), and Mr. F. A. Frost. when he looked dangerous. Half-time :—Runcorn, two , ATTEMPTED TILL ROBBERY AT A PUBLIC HOUSE.— goals one try ; Broughton, nil. On the restart Houghton mulled and let the visitors John Robert Bradley, labourer, of Burtonwood, was in. A penalty relieved but Wilson and Hogg got away, charged with attemptin_g to steal 6s. 9d. from a till and a furious attack was levelled at the bottom corner, drawer at the Elm Tree Inn, Eturtonwood, on February Three times the leather was all but carried across. I 26th, the money of Jane Kelsall.—Lavinia Hammett, Nothing but a superb defence could have withstood the servant at the Elm Tree Inn, stated that on the day in question she left the bar, leaving the prisoner and a strain, Jolley being instrumental in averting thedtuager man named Potter there. The till drawer was shut. Moran next had a grand opportunity, a knock on spoil. When she returned she saw prisoner leaning over the ing the movement. The ball was rushed back and a counter with his hand in the till. She called for Mrs. throw across enabled Woodhead to drop on the leather, Kelsall and he ran away.—P.C. Bateman spoke to arrest- the appeal foa palpable offside play being disallowed. ing prisoner, who now said he was simply 'leaning over S. James failed at goal. Runcorn broke away after this the counter."—The Bench inflicted a fine of 10s. or seven but fine kicking by S. Jones enabled the visitors to days' imprisonment. Prisoner was also fined ss. for press. The visitors continued to gain possession but drimkenness at Buttonwood on February 28th. Moran by a pod tackle converted the play to the Rangers 25. From the scrummage Jolley gathered and HELPED HIMSELF.—Edward Potter of Buttonwood, ' was charged with stealing one pint of beer, of the value ; of 20., from the Elm Tree Inn, Buttonwood, on kicked another splendid goal. Runcorn kept up the February 26th. John KelSall, son of the licenisee,stated pressure a fine Wilk away by Moran and Price, nearly piercing the defence. Richardson nearly crossed , the line, fine kicking by Houghton keeping the visitors that his mother refused to serve the prisoner because penned in their quarters. Moran let the visitors in, she thought he had had quite enough. Witness had Butterworth redeeming the failure with a brilliant dash occasion to leave the bar, and on returning directly afterwards he saw prisoner drawing a pint of beer from with the pack at his heels, Wilson grassing him in time. Moran next tried to pop over but s the tap.—P. C. Bateman stating that on apprehending line with a clear , prisoner the latter said: "I have drawn it and I intend line before him. Jones and Jolley were next conspicu- lous with keen tackling, a further good, combined to drink it." Inspector Forster remarked that pris- oner might have placed the proprietress in a very effort being broken up by Jolley. Even play continued awkward position, if he had been found by the police to the close. Final : Runcorn, three goals, one_ try ; I under the Influence of drink, with beer In front of Broughton, one try. ! him.—Prisoner was fined ss. and costs. MINOR CASES.—John Swindells, asleep in charge of two horses and a lurry at Poulton on the 18th ult., was fined 10s., and James Farrell, drunk and disorderly at Penketh, on the Ist March, was fined ss. and costs.— Joseph Matthews and James Donnolley, of Buttonwood were each fined 3s. 6d. and Thomas Sullivan, who did not appear, was ordered to pay 8s 6d. for gaming with coins at Buttonwood.</p>
        <p>Other Soaps</p>
        <p>are said to save labour in washing,</p>
        <p>and wear on the clothes; but only Fels-Naptha returns your money, if you don't like it. Money talks, and talks true.</p>
        <p>I Atthe Tullamore Assizes, on Tuesday, Peter Birm ing ham, a, farmer, Wati indicted for the murder of his father Ballybrien, King's County, on Christmas night. Evi- dence was given that the two men had been drinking , and quarrellinr, for some days, and that the prisoner was found standing over the elder man, whose head was covered with wounds that proved fatal. A verdict of manslaughter was returned, and the prisoner was sen- tenced to ten years' penal servitude.</p>
        <p>FELS-NAPTHA 39 Wilson Street London EC</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>OTHER OFFICIALS.</head>
        <p>OTHER OFFICIALS.</p>
        <p>The other officials were elected as follows :—Sub- captain of the first eleven, Mr. James Callon; captain of the second eleven, Mr. Edward Pierpoint ; match secretary, Mr. W. H. Podmore ; bowling club secretary, Mr. Thomas Cooper; captain of the bowling club, Mr. R. T. Fairclough; tennis club secretary, Mr. J. B. Isaac, jun. ; auditor, Councillor Tinnion. The following committee to represent all sections of the club was elected :—Cricket section, Messrs. J. W. Bibby, J. Callon, E. Pierpoint and J. Gibbon; bowling section, Messrs. R. H. Daintith, H. H. Evans, W. Gill, and Dr. Ferguson; tennis section, Mr. C. G. Stone. The SECRETARY proposed the adoption of new rules .to comply with the Licensing Act. Councillor PIERPOINT seconded and the alterations were agreed to. Mr, W. H. GRIMSDALE was elected a new member of the club and the meeting concluded with a vote of thanks to the Chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>BABY'S FUTURE</head>
