      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>MARCH 6th 1903,</p>
        <p>Mr. CHAMBERLAIN AN ALTERNATIVE is much the over- GOVERNMENT. praised man of his</p>
        <p>th time, but almost all ,aci_ ge, b it must be admitted, work together for his vantaon• The fact that in the Supplementary Zot* 'Mates, on the discussion of which the House it Commons entered on Monday the Colonial coronary's South African tour is down for a sum nf Z 2,200, is gaining for him a kind of praise to •hieh he is most surely not entitled. "Why," it lc, remarked, "he has pacified four colonies for two elonsand guineas ! " How far the colonies have "(len pacified remains still to be proved. Present !iglu are not nearly so satisfactory as they might no• but the idea that Mr. Cue.3thERLAIN'S trip to South Africa has only cost £2,200 is wildly absurd. lae sum asked for in the Supplementary Esti- Inates is merely a little addition to a much more 'vast expenditure. The Colonial Secretary did not go to South Africa to practice thrift, or he Would not have gone in a man-of-war, and he Would not have travelled from place to place in special trains. It will probably be necessary to put two noughts after the first figure 2 before' the total cost of Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S visit to South Africa is stated with an approach to precision. ITot two thousand guineas, but a quarter of a Million pounds will be required from first to last, it is probable, before the bill run up in connection With the "great mission S' has been fully paid. To get credit by a lucky accident or an ingenious r use Icr only having cost the country £2,200 must be ..,..I•cgarded as a piece of singular good fortune. 110 re is something further indicative of having %en born under a lucky star in the fact that Mr. .°4AMBERLAIN has been some 7,000 miles away Whilst one or another of his colleagues has been falling into public disfavour. His reputation h is .A..gnne up very much in proportion as that of other 'inisters has gone down. Mr. BALFOUR rernarksi Tether sharply the other day that it land not be imagined because Mr. CHAMBER- -14111. Was in South Africa that he has not been nonsulted. He hinted at telegraphs and cables, was obviously anxious that the Colonial 'coretary shall be associated in the public mind With the doing of the Ministry; but the public Will tak3 another view of the case. It will refuse to believe that Mr. CHAMBERLAIN can have had anything to do with the Venezuelan " mess." It Ili. 11 decline to identify him with the obvious di.4. „inclination of the Government to prosecute Mr. "airr.uan Wright. Ic will clear him of coin- Olieity in the failures and follies of Mr. BEODRICK, Will Come back to find the Government drifting hither and thither without much will or purpose and secretlyldespised by many of his OWD supporters And if he keeps his hearing on the alert he will toon learn that his homecoming has been cona-</p>
        <p>dently anticipated as the only means of saving the Ministry from disaster. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN may or may not possess all the fine, business like, and statesmanlike qualities with which he is at present being credited. Bat the interesting point at the moment is that the Ministry should during his absence have been discovered to be so dependent upon hirn for whatever repu - tation it may possess. Since the corarnencernenl of the present session it has become painfully apparent that the Government Front Bench is not even a good average of the Conservative benches in generals and yet it is filled by gentlemen who have told us how they were driven into "com- pulsory self -approv ti," and how there is no practi- cable alternative to their continuance in office. We turn to the Opposition front bench and we see men of experience in all the branches of statecraft. The financier who carried the greatest Budget of late days, the most capable of recent Home Secretaries, a level-headed all round statesman like Sir HENRY CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN, with Mr. JOHN MORLEY and Sir EDWARD GREY and Sir RO,DERT REID and Sir HENRY FOWLER, and Mr. SYDNEY BUXTON. Surely such men are these with the possible addition of others like Mr. LLOYD GEORGE: Mr. FLETCHER MOULTON, or Mr. W. S. ROBSON, are a practical alternative to the very weak team of Mr. BALFOUR, even when Mr. CHAMBERLAIN, the one alleged strong man of the Ministry, is at home. What the Liberal Front Bench lacks is not ability, but agreement. If those who compose it could agree on a common policy of resistance to the Government, as should now be easy enough, the country would soon con- sider an alternative to the present administration, not only to be practicable but exceedingly desir- able.</p>
        <p>It has almost escaped notice that this month brings before its close the tercentenary of a most important event in English history. It was on March :24th, 1603, that King James I. succeeded to the Throne of England, and the northern and southern kingdoms were bound together by the link of a common Sovereignty. Perhaps too much has been made of centenaries and millenaries of late, or the event might have had a celebration of some kind. Another explanation of the neglect may lie in the fact that the Stuarts are still most unpopular, in spite of the efforts of the legitimists to persuade us that they gave us the only great Sovereigns this kingdom has known up to the present occupant of the Throne and his immediate predecessor. Certainly James I. was not in his time a much-ioved King. In Scotland he had passed as a poet, to the English he appeared a pedant. The union of the Crowns also was by no :means liked on either side of the border. Scotsmen had at first regarded the annexation of England as a revenge for the con- quest of their own country by Edward 1., and thousands of them flocked into London,to sow the seeds of that prejudice which was do forcibly expressed long afterwards by Dr. Johnson. When the mischief was done there came disillusion and within four years Caledonians found them- selves with surprise described Jas mean and beggarly persons, who were an embarrassment to their King and brought their country into contempt. Even the long-deferred Parliamentary unim. of Scotland and England met with public disapproval in either country. Those ancient rivalries and misunder- standings are, of course, at an end, absorbed by a larger patriotism and the feeling that what has happened has been for the great good of both. Still, without any thought of envy, it may be rs- markable as a curious coincidence that this ter- centenary finds the highest places in Church and State, the Premiership and the two Archbishoprics held by Scotsmen proud of their land of origin. -</p>
        <p>An editor in the Western States announces his policy in the following prospectus:—"Our aim—tell the truth though the heavens take a tumble. Our paper—Of the people,for the peonle,and paid for by the people. Our religion—Orthodox, with a firm belief in hell for delinquent subscribers. Our motto—Take all in sight and rustle for more. Our policy—To love our friends and brimstone our enemies. If thinc enemy smite thee on the cheek swipe him with haste and dexterity at the butt of his most convenient ear. What we advocate— One country, one flag, and one wife—at a time. Our object—To live in pomp and splendour."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
