      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>SOUSA AND HIS BAND. Concert at Warrington. Mr. J. P. Sousa and his famous band gave a concert in Parr Hall, Wiarrington, on Wednesday evening. AN IMPRESSION.</head>
        <p>SOUSA AND HIS BAND. Concert at Warrington. Mr. J. P. Sousa and his famous band gave a concert in Parr Hall, Wiarrington, on Wednesday evening. AN IMPRESSION.</p>
        <p>One expects, midis expected, to be impressed with Sousa and his band. The reputation of this famous musical combination, of course, precedes it, as do huge pictorial counterfeits of the great Sousa. The " March King " looks at one through his rince nez from every hoarding., and all this tends to create the necessary impression, which is doubled and trebled when the night of the concert arrives. There is, noticeable, first of all, a huge audience which has overflowed on to the spacious platform; and then the arrival of the band in penny numbers" rivets our attention. Instruments strange, vast, and weird begin to make their appearance. Some of them remind us of our old friend Major," the borough fire engine, another looks suspiciously like a Maxim-Nordenfeldt, and others quite put to shame " really, truly, twirly-whirly eel " in their fearsome tortuousness. BY and by the fifty or more performers have arrived, and after emitting sundry uncouth sounds from their various instruments prepare for • action. In comes Sousa, bows quickly to the applauding audience,seizes the baton, and in almost one movement starts off the band with a crash like a salvo of artillery. There is nothing at all ordinary about the performance which follows. One pays threepence for a penny programme —and the impression that we are in the presence of the mighty deepens. Sousa does nothing to dispel the motion. He cOnducts with a nonchalant haughtiness which is effective even from behind. His body inclines sideways and his carefully- gloved left hand beseeches the band to moderate itself in some andante passage; he jumps into action as the trumpets blare out brazen defiance,and anon wields his baton with a dainty" side-stroke suggestive of the stroller beheading thistles with his cane. The band plays brilliantly, and no sooner is one selection com- pleted, and the applause has hardly reached its full volume, than Sousa swings off with an encore, which he just as rapidly follows with a second. We notice there are five items on the programme prior to the interval. They are all "encored", whether the audience likes or not—it appears to " like "—but Sousa is adamant in never giving more than two encores or one piece. The band itself is a wonderful creation. Corps after corps of instrumentalists file into a, tune one after another with more than military precision. Occa- sionally a company of trombonists rise like one man and blare out their loyalty to " Imperial Edward." This is appreciated, so the trombonists repeat the performance, this time assisted by an equal number of cornetists. Together they shiver the atmosphere. At another time the drummer indulges in a crescendo-diminuendo solo which makes one quake with the dread of another hurricane. Finally Sousa and his band describe the "Chase of the Lion." It is a wild movement. The king of beasts is appar- ently getting considerably hustled. He roprs several times, (it is our accomplished friend the r drummer again), he is finally overtaken and then the bang of the gun proclaims his end, while the audience jumps with the realism of the whole thing. This concludes a two hours' per- formance, and we depart, taking our impression away with us. We do not doubt the greatness of Sousa and his band. They would blow any competitors off the earth, and their performance has a fine Yankee flavour. Somehow the final and lasting recollection is not of music, however, but of having had more than our money's-worth, and being well satisfied on that account.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES. EXHIBITION OF GAS STOVES AT RI-NCORN.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES. EXHIBITION OF GAS STOVES AT RI-NCORN.</p>
        <p>As will be seen from au advertisement in another column, an exhibition of the well known Wilson gas cooking and heating stoves will be opened in Bethesda school room, Vicar-street, Runcorn, on Tuesday next and will continue until Friday. A special feature of the exhibition will be a series of popular cookery lec- tures to be delivered by Mrs. C. S. Pitcher, principal of the Streatham School of Cookery, London, at 3 and 7.30 p.m. daily. Gas consumers are given a cordial invitation to attend the exhibition and see the gas cookers in operation. There will be no charge for i on. KING'S SCHOLARSHIP EXAMINATION.</p>
        <p>Ten sat for the above examination from the Runcorn Pupil Teachers Centre with the following result :—Four gained Ist class certificates ; four gained 2nd Class Certificates ; one 3rd class certificate and one failure : First Class Certificates :—Martha Holt.. Granville-street Board School ; Bertha Davies, St. Michxls Church ; L. West, St. MichAs Church ; Eva M. Smith, Runcorn National. Second Class Certificates :—Blanche Jones. St. Michtels Church School, Margaret Hill, and May Hillage, Granville-street Board; Agnes White, Runcorn National. Third Class Certificates :—Alice Pownall, uncorn National School. RusconN's MINT BrAcK-LISTER.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Police Court on Saturday, before Messrs.. W. Timmins and E. Imison, Joseph Bushell, jun., 28, Nelson-street, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Nelson-street on Friday night. The evidence of P.C. 13ratt showed that hearing a dis- turbance in Nelson-street he went and ascertained that the prisoner had thrown his father and mother in the street. He was very drunk and was cursing.—The magistrates having decided to coin ict prisoner was charged with being an habitual drunkard and the Clerk (Mr. Johnson) produced the register showing the con- victions.—The Chairman committed prisoner for a month and said he would be put on the black-list.—ln explana- tion the Chairman said he would not be able to obtain intoxicating liquor for three years.—Prisoner : That will be a good iob for me. 1 will have a chance of living a better life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>DROWNING- FATALITY AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>DROWNING- FATALITY AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. Dobson, district coroner, conducted an inquiry at the Runcorn Police Station on Saturday, into the circumstances attending the death of Samuel Chorlton (GO years of age) a boatman, whose body was recovered from the _lron Ore Basin, No. 7 and S lock, of the Bridge- water Canal at Runcorn on Thursday evening. Mr. C. Dutton was foreman of the jury. The evidence showed that the deceased was mate of a narrow boat "Elba" which arrived in Runcorn on Wednesday and was "tied up" at six in the evening. Deceased went ashore and met a friend, Arthur Morris, with whom he had two pints of beer. He was perfectly sober and was seen passing the gates by William Hignett, a lock keeper at 11.25 p.m.—He was not afterwards seen alive and the body was recovered on Thursday evening.—A verdict of " Found Drowned" was returned.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION 0 FWATCHES TO ACTIVE SERVICE</p>
        <p>VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of a parade of the Runcorn Volun- teers at the Drill Hall, an Friday evening, those mem- bers of the Active Service Company and Imperial Yeomanry, who had not up to then received them, were presented with watches, subscribed for by the towns- p':,!ople in recognition of their services in South Africa. Each watch bore the inscription : " Presented to—by the inhabitants of Runcorn, on his return from active s'wvice in the Boer War in South Africa, 1899-01." The recipients were as follows : Troopers R. West, .1. Massey M. Mellor, T. 'Mellor, H. Lunt, E. Douglas, H. hill, and IV. Locker, and Privates W. Dale, S. Whitby, J. Boyle and T. P, Mort. Captain Lummis in making the presentations referred to the circumstances which called the men to the front. The twelve recipients were the last of the gallant band who had returned from serving King and Country. The watches would remind them of the appreciation of the townsfolk.—After the presentations the men were convivially entertained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>nu, County Council, through its officers, is determined to save the public front being imposed upon by purchas- ing broad of short weight. The prosecution of a local baker at the Petty Sessions resulted in a conviction and there can be no doubt that local bakers will have to be much more careful in future.</p>
        <p>The first " black lister " has been posted at Runcorn —a fact which will act as a salutary NVartling to all who are disposed to imbibe too freely of the cheery yet inebriating glass. In common with other towns tlicro appears to have been a decided falling off in the drunk cases at the Police Courts. Though bad for the police exchequer it is good for the public weal.</p>
        <p>There was re/411y little but formal business transacted at the Urban Council meeting. The only important question discussed touched on the Education Act, and it was adjourned for a week in order that additional in- formation might be procured. There can be no doubt that the Council acted wisely in not precipitately levy- ing a penny rate, but holding their hand to see, first,the game the County Council intend to play. Though Mr. Posnett displayed a little too much heat in pressing his point he was substantially right. It would be pre- mature to further discuss the future of the Technical Schools seeing that the Urban Council and the County Council do not at present quite know where they are.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>FOOTE AL L.</head>
        <p>FOOTE AL L.</p>
        <p>Salfo"rd were the visitors to Runcorn on Saturday, and were given a good game and a pretty severe best- hg. The only notable absentee on the home side was Richardson, whose place was taken by Walker. He made a fair substitute but missed forcingseveral impor- tant openings which should have led to results. Salford were completely outplayed at their own game, and the win was thoroughly deserved. Jolley played a marvel- lous game and seemed, indeed, to hold the entire team at bay. He was individually responsible for the down- fall of the "Reds." Price played a lasting game throughout and has developed a grand style all round. Moran did not shape at all brilliantly, but would be a, fine wing if he would only put a little grim determina- tion into his work. The visitors in the last half, screwed the packs splendidly, but they could not pierce that steel cordon forged by Houghton and his merry men.</p>
        <p>On Wednesdaythe Rangers of Broughton journeyed to Irwell-lane and met with disaster. Though slightly tame there were some brilliant and exciting incidents, the most. notable being a magnificent run by Butter- worth with all his opponents following his heels. Jolley played another wonderful game, being responsible for two beautiful dropped goals. His attack and defence were superb as indeed was the play of Butterworth, Price and Richardson. Houghton showed that his foot had not lost its cunning in finding touch and relieved the pressure in fine style over and over again. Forward the Rangers slightlyhadthe best of matters. Fielding, however, neutralised the determination of the attack by wretchedly poor play. At present "The Linnets" are going strong and will be a hard nut to crack by the best team in the Northern Union.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>:DRAUGHTS.</head>
