      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Examiner Friday, March 6, 1903</head>
        <p>The Examiner Friday, March 6, 1903</p>
        <p>"Mary," Mr. E.Worrall; recitation, "Adam and Mary," Miss E. Turner; song. (selected), Miss E. Clare; sketch, "On parade" (Tyme), Mark Tyme; banjo solo, "White Coons," Mr. Sam Ennion; song, Zuyder Zee," Miss L. Wood; song, "The old soldier," Mr. T. N, Johnson ; sketch, "Funniosities," Mark Tyme; whist- ling solo,' selected' Mr. T. Yates; song, "Home Sweet Home," Miss P. Humphreys; song,, "Flight of Ages," Mr. T. N. Johnson; humorous song, 'When Mrs. McKey rode a Bike," (by request) Mr. F. Ellison; violin solo, "Selected," Miss L. Temperley ; duet, "Singing Lesson," Miss Simkin and Mr. R. Morris; song, "Take it home and give it to the Baby," Miss N. Temperley ; humorous song." Selected," Mark Tyme. At the conclusion of the entertainment the usual vote of thanks was accorded the officials. Considerable credit devolves on Mr. Howard (secretary). who pioneered the scheme through in grand style from first to last. The cabs were kindly lent by Messrs. Yarwood and Booth. The piano was lent by Mr. J. W. Temper- ley, of Lowlands-road; the tobacco was given by the Imperial Tobacco Company and the accompanist was Mr. W. Cooke, F.R.C.O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>I The monthly meeting of the above Council was held lon Wednesday morning in the Council Chamber, Mr. S. Taylor in the chair. Present. —Messrs. Wilson, W. Handley, D. Bisbrown, T. Jones, W. Hewitt, J. J. Shaw, J. H. Salkell, R. H. Posnett, F. Wiswall, S. Walton, T. Dean, E. Gatley, J. W. Lightburn, with Mena. F. A. Lake (law clerk), E. Marshall (secretary), W. McDougall. (medical officer., THE HEALTH OF THE TOWN. The medical report showed that for the past month there were twenty-five deaths and thirty-six births. Last year there were twenty-one deaths and thirty-six births. The town continued free from small-pox, the last patient being discharged from the hospital that day. GIFT OF SWANS. THE IMPROVEMENT OF WESTON ROAD. The Highways and Buittling Committee recommended that a letter be sent to Mrs. Abel acknowledging the gift of land to enable Weston-road to be widened at its junction with Highland-road; that the offer of Mr.: Joseph B. Leach, Huyton Park, Liverpool, to give two ; swans for the lake on the Common be accepted. Thatl a notice be served on the Runcorn Gas Co. of the inten- tion of the Council to terminate the contract for street lighting on the 31st May next. Mr. Posnett, in moving the adoption of the abov minutes said that the town owed a small debt of grati- tude to Mrs. Abel for enabling a better and safer ap- proach to be made to Weston by giving the land. They! werealso grateful to Mr.Lea,ch for his gift of swans and I ; he hoped that the boys of the town would not in any way molest the birds when they arrived as they had a pecu- liar way of defending themselves. With reference to the contract with the gas company a new contract was 'being drawn up. The company had agreed to put up incandescent lights when the Council erected the ! necessary posts so that the town would be better lighted in the future. I Mr. Handley seconded and the minutes were con- firmed. ILE-APPOINTMENT OF THE OVERSEERS OF THE POOR. , On the motion of Messrs. Handley and Lightburn,Mr. I S. Taylor and Mr. E. Imison were re-appointed to the ; above positions. Both gentlemen were the recipients of laudatory speeches from the mover and seconder. SECONDARY EDUCATION, A PENNY RITE RECOMMENDED. , I The Technical instruction Committee reported that , under section 3 of the Education Act, 1902, the Council I has power to spend on secondary (including technical) , I education such a sum as shall not exceed the amount !which would be produced by a rate of one penny- in the pound. The'Council is responsible for all liabilities in ! connection with the work carried on in the Technical Institute at the appointed day on which the Education . Act will come into operation inthe County. In addition to providing money for scholarship purposes and to meet the liabilities on the appointed day, the com- mittee are of opinion that it is impossible to say what ; will be the position and requirements of the Institute during the coming year as to its income and expendi- ' ture.They therefore recommend that a rate of one penny in the pound be levied for secondary education pur- !poses. The committtee also reported that at present , there are twelve students in the secondary day ! school holding local scholarships; these expire at the close of the present school year, and the donations required to continue them are not likely to be forth- coming. Scholarships of the following character are I more desirable. There are four holders of Local • Science and Art Scholarships in the school. It is recommended that these be increased to eight, and it will probably be found desirable to increase them to twelve a year hence. To comply to the conditions of , the Board of Education the managers of the school I must contribute £5 each year in respect of each scholar . and lodge that sum annually with the board before the sth of April. For the first year of tenure of the Scholarship, the local contribution will be supplemented j Iby the board with a grant of £4; for the second year ! with a grant of .E 7 ; and for the third year with a grant ;of £lO. Contributions from a rate, if assigned by a I local authority for the purpose, meet the requirements ;of the board. The Committee therefore recommend ! that a sum of £lO be provided out of the Id. rate for : these scholarships. lln conjunction with the minutes a letter was read from the Clerk to the County Council stating that the Education Scheme Committee of the Council will meet 1 at Crewe on Thursday, sth March, anti that they will be Iprepared to receive in Conference, the representatives lof the Borough and Urban District Councils on the subject of the provision of Higher Education in the ; I County, and upon such other points arising upon the I administration of the Act as such representatives may ' desire to bring under the notice of the County Council, and asking for the appointment of two representatives if agreeable to the Council. Mr. Wilson moved the adoption of the above minutes! The reason, he said, why the Connell was asked to levy a penny rate was because the new authority did not come into power before the Ist July. The Council was responsible for the maintaining of the Institute up to that period and as there had to be no liabilities at the tune , of taking over, the expenses to that date Would have to be met. They could not at present tell what the expenses would be but if there was any surplus from the penny rate it could go in the general exchequer., The other recommendation was an entirely new de- parture. They asked for money out of the rates to sup- port the day school. There was a number of scholar- ships held in connection with the day school and twelve ; expired at the close of the present school year. Sixteen : scholarships had been created and maintained entirely by private subscription. But as 'a new state of things had come into existenee this year a number of scholar- ships would cease and some would refuse to subscribe any longer. They did not wtsh the school to sustain a loss and they wished to establish four other scholar- ships in addition to the four established last year. They thus wanted £2O for the four already running and £2O for the four new-ones to be established. Mr. Handley formally seconded. Mr. Dean asked whether the proposed penny rate would be in addition to the penny rate they already paid. Mr. Wilson: Oh! No! Mr. Posnett was sorry that he could not accept the recommendations. No arguments had been given the Council to induce them to yob the money. They had always taken a deep interest in the Technical schools and he did not think because the County Council were going to take over the schools that the townsfolk would cease to interest themselves in the schools. He thought it I better for the committee to take the recommendations !back until they had the report from the representatives who would be sent to Crewe. He did. not think it was at all wise for the committee to suggest that persons would withdraw the subscriptions and that the scholar- ships would have to be given up. It would be a great mistake if the Council voted the penny rate and would be an insult to the County Council. The wisest thing to do would be to hold their hands until they saw what the County Council were prepared to do. The rate should really go before the Finance Committee and be discussed with the other rates of the town. Mr. Dean seconded. They had no idea what the Council would do, and they should be very careful in taxing themselves. Mr. Lightburnesaid that the County Council would deal with primary education first and secondary educa- tion after, so that it was very improbable that the County Council would actually take the schools over on-the Ist -July. At present there were no facts for the County Council to work on. The schools would have to • be maintained, and if they did not levy the penny rate what was going to support the Technical Institute? Mr. Wilson: We should have to shut up. The Chairman: We have power to levy a penny rate any time? Mr. Lightburn said that if the rate were levied and , there was a surplua it could go into the general account. The Secretary pointed out that the money must be ' paid into the Board of Education before the sth April. ! They would have to be advertised and the examinations held before then. After a considerable amount of discussion it was ultimately resolved that the Council adjourn their meeting until Wednesday week. Messrs: Handley and Wilson with the secretary, (Mr. Marshall) were ap- pointed to attend the Conference at Crewe and after , the report was considered by the Council the Technical Instruction Committee's minutes would be further dis- cussed b? the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>AGED PERSONS TREAT AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>AGED PERSONS TREAT AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The annual aged Persons treat was held on Wednesday in the Public Hall Runcorn. Over four hundred persons sat down to tea their being twice tia( many females as males. The oldest person present was Mary ' Robinson, aged, 95, residing in Cavendish street Runcorn. It is pleasing to say that the old folks thoroughly enjoyed the repast the wants being attended to by a numerous staff of willing helpers. The following ladies presided over the tables : Mesdames Littlemore, Sweetman, H. Grounds, P. Leath- wood, Miss Beck, T. Leach, Jones, Collier, Ennion, S. Littler, Dykes, G. Howard, G. Kirkham, jun., Miss Seragg, Wood, R. Moore, Bellamy, Miss Millington, Byfield, Miss Broadhurst, Rhodes, Yates, Brocklebank, J. Shaw jun., T. Williams, Coventry, W. Shaw, jun., Weston, Carroll, Miss Clare, Luxton, Ellison, Miss Dobson, W. Coventry, T. Cooke, Riley, Miss Collins, Leathwood, A. R. Norman, Wyleman, Miss T. Lea, Walton, Wilding, Turner, Buckingham, McLennan; Bowyer, R. Morris, J. Timperley, J. N. Frith, W. Cooke, Miss Lunt, Miss Humphreys, and Douglas. At the after meeting Mr. S. Taylor ptesided, and he was supported by Messrs. T. Cooke, T. Wood, %V. Shaw, G. Howard (secretary), and other local gentlemen. In opening the proceedings the chairman said he was pleased to see so many faces present year after year. A great deal of interest was now being taken in that annual meeting. and it was a great .pleasure to those who got up the entertainment to see so many enjoying themselves. They all looked forward to it and there was not the slightest possibility of the Old Persons Treat ever falling through. There were now plenty of workers who were only too pleased to offer their ser- vices. He was afraid that two or three years ago there was rather a lack of interest in the movement but now it was going stronger than ever. (Applause). The following programme was afterwards submitted the majority of the artistes receiving encores. Song 4'3 tar‘of the twilight," Miss J. Waterworth; song</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>GILCHRIST LECTURE AT EARLESTOWN. " The Brain as the Apparatus of the Mind."</head>
        <p>GILCHRIST LECTURE AT EARLESTOWN. " The Brain as the Apparatus of the Mind."</p>
        <p>The fourth of the series of Gilchrist Lectures was</p>
        <p>delivered in the Town Hall, Earlestown by Dr. Alex Hill his subject being "The Brain as the apparatus of the Mind." As usual the hall was packed, Dr. Handy-</p>
        <p>_ , side occupying the chair.* FURTHER EXTENSIONS CONTEMPLATED. ; The Chairman briefly introduced the lecturer who re- At a meeting of the Water Special Sub-Committee I marked that the aim of the lecture was to trace as far held on Monday afternoon it was resolved that on the , as may he done in the existinz state of knowledge, the relation between the apparatus of mind—the brain— recommendation of the Water Committee the tender of I . ! and the processes which occur within the brain. In the Messrs Bennie and Thompson, contractors, Warrington be accepted for the laying of the new distribution ' year 1880 ,two eminent doctors 'a tttended lectures i having undertaken to complete the work with Paris by Dr. Gall and they thought that the mind and mains,they soul displayed the activities of the I.l:ain. Mind con- in a period satisfactory to the sub-committee. A letter was read from Messrs. Read and Waring with respect to ; 1 sists of a number of different organs and faeulties which i are assigned to it. One had only to got the head of p , the roper laying of the new mains. It was resolved someone and find out which part was prominent and I that Messrs. Bennie and Thompson be requested to give ; then see which of these faculties distinguished his an undertaking that they will employ experienced men ! : character. This we call the bulk. The manner in in the laying of the mains to the satisfaction of the ! ! ! which they search for these bulks or faculties Council's engineers. The Chairman reported upon his was somewhat haphazard. Fo: example what is the faculty that distinguishe the pickpocket interview with Mr. Leslie as to the obtaining of addi- tional land for the purpose of driving headings and i ! ' , Pickpockets are distinguished by acquisitiveness. Dr. read a letter from Mr. Leslie stating that he - 1 Gall had amongst his acquaintances a lady who was in was willing to let the Council have an additional 1 business as a milliner. He found that she was promin- acre of land on the north side. It was resolved that the Council be recommended to lease an acre of land ent about here—indicating a spot at the back of the ' head—this was called the bump of constructiveness. , from Lord Newton on the terms stated. The question , Then he looked at the head of a dog and found that a • of the appointment of a suitable man to superintend the prominent bump in the same position denoted destruct- : laying of the new mains was left to the Chairman iind . ' i iveness. They would always find that men were in- ' the Water Engineer. - I variably distinguished by that mark, viz., a small lump! Dr. J. Campbell Brown, the County Analyst certified just above the nape of the neck. At a result of this, it I that the quality of the water from the 190 feet heading ' was pointed out to Dr. Gall that the murderer Thirskill lin the red sandstone, was "excellent," and Dr. Percy I had a bump of benevolence b3caase he had once pre- I Frankland, F.R.S., Birmingham, certified that the "water ' seated a friend with 10s. These bumps can never be iis of the most excellent quality for drinking, washing j displaced. But he was not going to take up their time : and manufacturing purposes; indeed it would be difli- i with a lecture about phrenology. He could very well . cult to find it water possessing a composition better j leave that part to the chairman. Mind is the display suited to the purposes of a general town's supply." The minutes were approved on the proposition of Mr. lof the enormous activities of the brain. One could get ! Ithrough life very comfortably without giving the brain : C. B. F. 13orron. any work. Looking out of the window is not thinking. i APPOINTMENT OF MEDICAL OFFICER.</p>
        <p>It Is the exercise of-the memory. it is with the brain that we think. The senses are the windows of the mind. From them you look out into the world. The messages which from earliest infancy afford you the sense of smell, taste, and hearing, Sze., have got the mind in a state of activity. As a matter of course, a person, who had no sense of smell, nor taste, etc. could not have a mind. With reference to the sense- organs the lecturer remarked that the stimulation of a sense-organ gives rise to a nerve impulse, which on reaching the brain produces a sensation. The quieter the room and the darker the more likely were they to go •to sleep. He was going to take them for a trip round the minds mill. If there was no chance of seeing the works in action they would run in some Sunday when the fires were out. They were not going...to dis- turb the manager of the mill. The thoughts are the weaving together of experiences. As proof of the various organs possessed by animals, etc., the lecturer exhibited a photograph of a jelly fish which possesses eight sense organs. if seven of these eight organs were taken away, and only one left, and then a pin was stuck in this remaining one, .the jelly fish would swing round what is called the clapper, and endeavour to remove the offending pin. If ,the jelly fish were put in a tub of salt water and thensome vinegatadded to the water,the movernentsof the fish would be hurried, and he would put as far a distance as possible between his one remaining organ and the portion of the water which was contaminated. The frog is very much the same .as the jelly fish in this respect. sit is possible to cut away the whole of the brain of the frog and yet if he is put in a- wet moss and fed on a worm a day, he can be kept alive for a couple of years. During the whole of that period froggy will not move however; he becomes a mere machine. If he is put in a basin of water he will swim round and round until he appears to be completely exhausted. By strok- ing his back between finger and thumb froggy experi- • vices a queer sensation which forces him to emit a , croak and as the croak of every frog is entirely different in tone, by doing this to seven or eight of these animals, they can be played upon just like an orchestra. in ex- ' hibiting a photograph of the brain of a man who had , died at the age of 22 years, the lecturer explained that although this nutn's head was fully developed,when the brain was examined only a very small portion of it was found to be developed at all. The rest of the brain was nothing more than a large bag of fluid. To call that mm an idiot would be an insult to idiocy and idiots generally. He was nothing more than a log. For 22 years he lay in bed and never even knew his own mother. The only portion. of . his brain that was _</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Highways and Sanitary Com- mittee on Monday afternoon, applications were con- sidered for the appointment of a medical officer of , health. It was resolved that the Council be recom- mended to appoint .Mr. Harold Ernest Watkins as medical officer of health for the district for a period up to the 25th day of March 1904, with a salary at the rate of £75 per annum, such appointment to be subject to the approval of the Local Government Board and further that a moiety of such salary be claimed out of the County funds. The appointment was approved by the Council. Dr. Harold E. Watkins was called into the room and made acquainted with the decision of the Council by the Chairman. ! Dr. Watkins thanked the Council for the honour they had done hint in selecting him to fill the post held for so many years by his father. He should always en- deavour to do his duty as his conscience directed hint and he hoped the Council would have reason to be satisfied with his work. (Haar, hear.) I• THE PROPOSED 1 ISOLATION HOSPITAL. The following report of ! the Isolation Hospital sub-Committee of the 2nd February was received and adopted at the meeting of the Highways and Sanitary . Committee. (a) The Clerk reported that he had \Vritten to the Haydock and Golborne Urban District Councils, ' asking whether they would be willing to join this Council in the provision of an' Isolation Hospital for the joint districts, and that he had been informed that the matter Would be brought before those Councils at their next meetings. (b) The Clerk also reported that Lord Newton's agent had informed him that Messrs. R. Evans and Co. had written him objecting to the pro- posed sitp for the erection of the Hospital, and that he , wished the matter to be again considered by this com- mittee. It was resolved that the Clerk inform Mr.A.C. Leslie (Lord Newton's agentl that the committee havo Carefully considered Messrs. Evans and Co.'s objections to the proposed site, but that they cannot see any justi- , Rattle reason for altering their decision. It was also ' resolved that the Clerk make formal application to Lord Newton for the land required. THE SMALLPDX CASE. ' The monthly report of the Inspector of Nuisances was read before the Highways and Sanitary Committee,from ; which it appeared, that the district was free from small pox, the one patient having been declared well; • and further, that proper means had been taken for dis- infecting and cleansing in connection with the case. SECONDARY ROADS. •</p>
        <p>developed was thit, /int which is assigned to no par- The Highways and Sanitary Committee recommended Ocular sense. The brain is made up of about 600 that Rob-lane, Vista-road and Wargrave-road be added million nerve cells and their branches. Every one of to the schedule of secondary roads; and that applica- these cells and their branches are always working and tion be made to the County Council Main Roads and from them the messages from the sense organs are des- Bridges Committee forspecial grants for improvements patched to be collected by these nerve cells. Every in the following secondary roads, Market-street, Vista- message which passes from the nerve interwork, makes ' road, and Rob-lane. it easier for another message of the same kind to pass ; The minutes were approved of. through. This applies to the man taking a first glass of MR. CARNEGIE AND LIBRARIES. intoxicants, who says he will not touch afterwards. The ! At, a meeting of the General Purposes Committee a silly fool does not realise that that message passing letter was read from. the General Manager of the through its particular portion of the brain makes it far London and North-Western Railway, stating that the easier for the next to follow. - - ! Company had definitely decided for the present not to . .</p>
        <p>During the lecture oxy-hydrogen lantern pictures were exhibited on a screen. A vote of thanks to the lecturer was proposed by Mr. Collingwood and seconded by Mr. Speakman, this closing the proceedings.</p>
        <p>• cover the footbridges at the Earlestown Passenger • Station.—The Clerk reported that he had received a communication from Mr. Carnegie's Private Secretary; j in which he stated that Mr. Carnegie would take up the I matter of Public Libraries on his return to Scotland in May.</p>
