      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>NEWTON COUNCIL. Appointment of Medical Officer of</head>
        <p>NEWTON COUNCIL. Appointment of Medical Officer of</p>
        <p>Health.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Newton-in-Makerfleld Urban Dis- trict Council was held in the Town Hall, Earlestown, on Monday evening. Mr. W. Collingwood, J.P., pre- sided and there were also present: Messrs. C. B. P. Borron, C.C., E. L. Bent, J. W. Emmett, IP., E. T. Evans,W. Gilmour, T. Ormand, Dr. Patterson, N. Siddeley, J. Simpson, A. Smith, W. Thompson, F. .T. Wood with C. Cole (clerk), A. Bowes (surveyor) and R. T. Surtees (surveyor).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>LATE DR. WATKINS.</head>
        <p>THE LATE DR. WATKINS.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Dr. H. E. Watkins thanking the Council on behalf of the relatives of the late Dr. Watkins for their kind resolution of sympathy. THE POOR QUALITY OF GAS.</p>
        <p>A letter was read before the Gas Committee from the superintendent of the Viaduct works complaining of the poor quality of the gas supplied to the works on the 11th and 12th inst. also the clerk's reply thereto, in which he stated 0114 owing to the error of a workman the gas was inferior in quality on the dates mentioned, and that means had at once been taken for its improve- ment. It was resolved that the Gas Extensions Sub- Committee be requested to hold a special meeting to consider the question of the poor quality of the gas and the subject of theocomplaint from the Viaduct works, and to report to this committee at its next meeting. The minutes were passed on the proposition of Mr. Thompson.</p>
        <p>THE WATER SUPPLY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>THE EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Education Committee it was resolved that the Council be recommended to apply to the Education Committee of the County Council, for that committee to delegate their powers under Part 111. of the Education Act, 1902,t0 a committee for the area of this Urban District.</p>
        <p>REPRESENTATIVE GOVERNORS ON EDUCATIONAL BODIES.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr. Thompson it was resolved that Messrs. Borron, Emmett, Ormand and Wood be appointed representative governors on the governing body for the administration of the following endowment schemes, viz.: The Wilmick Grammar School, the Dean School Exhibition Charity, and the Lowton Endowed School. The Council then rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>LBY A CORRESPONDENT].</p>
        <p>What with burglaries and rumours of burglaries, th district has during the past week been greltly excited The burglary at the Ca-operative Society's place wa of a peculiarly impudent character. The thieves, fo there were apparently two, discarded their own clothes which were of a dilapidated character,and arrayed them- selves in new garments, but they were unable to coin- plete their robing, ()living to the shoe department having been removed. A number of other articles were placed ready for removal, but the marauders had evidently been disturbed, and tiler totijd not get them away. The police have, up to the present, no clue, but they are on the look out for likely characters arrayed in new clothes.</p>
        <p>The District Council on Monday proceeded with the election of a Medical Officer of Health in succession to the late Dr. Watkins, who held the post for many years. There were six applicants, but only one of them, (Dr_ Latham), held a diploma of M.O.H. Dr. 'Latham is o member of the Council, and had he succeeded in obtain- ing the post, he would have had to resign his position on the Connell.</p>
        <p>The majority of th 3 vote 3, h3wever, were cast" for Dr. H. Watkins, and he, of course, received the appoint- ment, which carries with it a renumeration of £75 a year. It is to be hoped that Dr. Watkins will not con- sider the post a sinecure, but that he will stir things up a little and take steps to remove some of the offensive smells which abound in parts of the district and which are, without doubt, detrimental to public health.</p>
        <p>The date for the District Council elections is approach- ing, and the question for the ratepayers in the different wards to consider, is whether their representatives have properly represented them or not. The question can with truth be answered in the negative so far as War- grave, Crow-lane. and Newton Wards are concerned, for it is pretty certain that neither of the three retiring Coimcillors have given that attention to their duties, during their three years of office which the ratepayers have a right to expect. They have only just kept them- selves eligible, but if the ratepayers are well advised, they will have nothing further to do with them. In, justice to the ratepayers they ought to retire and make room for others who have a right conception of their obligations,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Earlestown managed to secure two points on Satur- day at the expense of Rochdale Town. Until noon there was every expectation of a good game played under auspicious circumstances, but just about the time for the kick-off rain began to fall, and during the progress of the game increased to a perfect deluge. This interfered greatly with the attendance of spectators, and also with the play, which was of an uninteresting character. Both sides, however, did their best to score, the advantage - being with Rochdale who played down hill. Pressure was brought to bear on both defences, but in a mull round the Earlestown goal Leigh handled the ball, and a penalty was awarded the visitors. Partin,gton inter- cepted the ball as it was sent in, but owing to its wet and slippory condition, he was unable to retain his hold of it, and striking the upright it rebounded into the net. It was a grand effort on the part of the goalkeeper but the rain was certainly responsible for his failure to prevent the ball entering the net.</p>
        <p>Earlestown have now got together a very fair team, and if the Committee can only refrain from indulging in their choppingand changing, they may be able to obtain a good position in the League table. They have been very unforttuate, however, with their home matches, in the matter of weather, and it is likely their ffitonces will suffer. On Saturday they have Chorley as visitors, when it is hoped the weather will be more pro- pitious not only for the players, but for the spectators.</p>
        <p>Newton had another bad day OR Saturday. They paid a visit to Chester, but, although the game was very evenly contested, they failed to secure the victory.</p>
