      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>INFLUENZA INCREASING.</head>
        <p>INFLUENZA INCREASING.</p>
        <p>The Influenza Epidemic is again spreading over the Country. The visitation is of a very prostrating nature, and the disease is certainly on the increase. The origin of the disease or rather the circumstances which bring it about is as much a mystery as ever. You cannot trifle with Influenza. It undermines the Nervous System to a greater extent than almost any other disease, and it is Pneumonia that kills, and Pneumonia strikes the man who imagines he has only amild form of Influenza, and can go about the street just as usual. It can hardly be doubted that the prevalence of Chest and Lung' Complaints must be ascribed to the baneful influence of Influenza, whose after-effects so frequently take this form. It was a .bad day for this Country when this malignant scourge first visited our shores. Year after year it has never since failed to revive at its due time, and to extort its tribute of human life. Having observed its ravages, and its dreadful effects upon the constitution on the occasion of its former visits, we cannot but regard its recurrence with much fear, as a great many have not yet, if they ever will,regain their former health. As a preventative, we know of nothing equal to Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. A few strong doses taken in time have often provedeffectual in ward- ing off attacks of this Malady, and always succeed in mitigating their severity when a patient 13 under their influence. Do you suffer from Chills, alternating witty Sadden Flushes. Severe Head-ache. •accomnanied by difficulty of breathing, and symptoms of Catarrh, Pains in the Chest, under and between the shoulders, and a stiffness and soreness of the Muscles,,Loss of Appetite and Nervousness. If you suffer from any of these symp- toms, know that they aro the forerunners of Influenza, and it behoves you to resort at once to a course of Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. By common consent Patients everywhere admit that they feel more depressed and miser- able after an attack of Influenza than while under its influence, and more than they feel after an attack of any other illness. The best way to banish these miser- able feelings is to take Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. We can strongly and confidently recommend this preparation, as it has treated several cases of Influenza, Heavy Colds, Extreme Weakness, and Langour, with greater success than any other known Remedy. We may also add that as a Remedy for Indi- gestion, Sleeplessness, Low Spirits, Neryousness and Loss of Appetite, Gwilym Evans' Quinine. Bitters stands unequalled. It is sold in bottles 2s. 9d. and 4s. ficl., by all Chemists and Patent Medicine Vendors, or sent direct, carriage free, for the above prices, by the sole proprietors: Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company Limited Llanelly,SouthWales. Avoid imitations. See the name " Gwilym Evans" on Label, Stamp, and Bottle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON POLICEMEN ATTACK ED-SOLDIERS IN</p>
        <p>TROUBLE.</p>
        <p>" Two soldiers stationed at Orford Barracks named George Taylor and George Kirk were charged before the War- rington magistrates on Monday with having been drunk and disorderly in Crown and Septre Yard,Buttermarket- street, and with assaulting Police Constable Sankey, on Saturday night. The constable stated that at a quarter to eleven on Saturday night he heard a disturbance in the Crown and Septre-yard. He saw the prisoners there in the centre of a large crowd. Kirk had his belt off and appeared to have been fighting. He advised them to go home, and they refused. Taylor struck him in the mouth and Kirk struck him on the head with his belt, knocking him down. He also struck him with the belt several times while he was on' the ground. He blew his whistle for assistance and a young man named Shaw got hold of Taylor, who attempted to escape.P.C.'s Cooper and Metcalfe came upon the scene and the pris- on3rs were locked up. They were violent all the way to the Police station.—John Shaw, Orchard-street,and P.C. Cooper (36) gave evidence—An officer from the barracks stated that neither of the men had been con- victed for drunkenness. I Taylor had been six years in the army but Kirk had only recently joined the Militia —Taylor, who was wearing a medal with several bars, was fined 10s. including costs, and Kirk was sent to goal fora month. Shaw was awarded 2s. 6d. for assisting the police. James Hopkins, of no settled abode,was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Peter-street on Saturday afternoon and with assaulting P.C.'s Sweeney and Stewart. Sweeney stated that he saw the prisoner in Peter-street on Saturday. He was drunk and behav- ing in a very disorderly manner. He stopped two men with bicycles and swore at them. Witness took him into custody, and he then became very violent. He kicked him several times on the legs, and tupped him with his head. He threw himself on the around. Witness got the assistance of P.C.'s Millington and Stewart, and the prisoner kicked Stewart. He made use of obscene language on the way to the Police Station.—P.C. Stewart corroborated, and said they had to put the prisoner on the floor an take his boots off, he wad so violent. He kicked witness several times.— Prisoner, who made his 29th appearance, was sent to gaol for three months with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Minor Cases.</head>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>3losnay.—Before Mr. F. Monks (presiding), Alderman Smethurst, and Messrs. Birchall, Hayward and Thornton. CRUELTY TO HORSES.—Jaines Burgess, Cabbage Hall Farm, Antrobus, was charged with working a horse whilst in an unfit condition on February 12th, and his father, William Burgess, was charged with causing it to be worked. P.C. Cooper said he saw the younger defendant driving the horse, which was attached to a spring cart, in Wilderspool-road,near the bridge. The horse was very lame, but the defendant said it was ''all right when he left 'home." Inspector Print and Mr. Dixon, veterinary surgeon, said the horse was suffering from contracted tendons and overshot fetlock. It was unfit for work. Defendants were each fined ss. and costs.—William Hewitt, of Appleton-street, Widnes, was summoned for cruelty to a horse. Sereant Cheney stated that he saw the horse in Museum-street and it could scarcely walk. It was in an emaciated condition and defendant promised to destroy it. He paid £2 10s. for it two years ago.—A fine of 10s. was imposed. DISORDER IN CA IRO-STREET.—Five boys named William Potter, 1, Moss-street, Ebenezer Rowlands, 17, Alder-lane, and John William Warburton, Thomas Bradshaw and Robert Barrett, all of Winwick-road,were each fined 2s. 6d. for making loud and unseemly noises in Cairo-street on February 23rd.—P.C. Clough proved the case and said the boys were shouting and "singing" and behaving in a disorderly manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>THEFT OF A WATCH AT EARLESTOWN. Sentence of Imprisonment.</head>
        <p>THEFT OF A WATCH AT EARLESTOWN. Sentence of Imprisonment.</p>
        <p>James H. .Corrigan, a Liverpool l man, was charged at the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday before Colonel Ridgway (presiding) and Mr. F. A. Frost, with stealing from the dwelling-house, 9, Wellington. street, Earlestown, on August 23rd, 1902, a silver lever watch, of the value of £2. the property of John Tins-. ley, a milk dealer. He pleaded not guilty. Prosecutor stated that the watch was hanging on a nail over the mantelpiece in the kitchen on the morn- ing in question. At one o'clock in the afternoon he found a piece of the watch chain lying on the floor. The watch had disappeared. Sarah Insley, wife of the prosecutor, said that during the morning she left the shop for a few minutes, leaving the door open, and went across the road. PrisoneName to her and offered a book for sale, but she declined to buy it and he went away. Fred William Brown, a booking clerk at Earlestown Railway Station, gave evidence as to booking the prisoner by the 1.9 p.m. train to St. Helens on the day in question..</p>
        <p>Ciswald Birchall, assistant to James Birchall, pawn- broker, St. Helens, said the prisoner pawned the watch at the shop on the 23rd August. Witness advanced him 13s. on it. The prisoner said he wanted money as he had a bill to pay. P.C. Hughes, Earlestown, said he made inquiries into the matter, and ascertained that the watch had been pawned. He received the prisoner into custody as he was leaving Walton Gaol on the 28th alt. When charged with the offence the prisoner replied "I shall say nothing about the matter until I get before the magistrates." _ _</p>
        <p>rrrisoner denied the offence, but admitted two previous convictions for theft against him. Ho also admitted that he had been before the magistrates for having attempted to commit suicide, and had been fined for iirunkenness.</p>
        <p>He was committed to gaol for two months.</p>
        <p>Captain Percy Scott, of H.M.S. Terrible fame, after some months of comparative inactivity on half-pay, is to assume the command of the guhnery ship Excellent at Portsmouth next month.</p>
        <p>At Clare Assizes, on Wednesday night, Michael Belian was indicted for the murder of his brothe: at Arman, West Clare, by shooting. The jury returned a 'verdict of "Manslaughter," and he was sentenced to imprisonment for life. Justice Gibson said that the crime was 1, urder.</p>
        <p>On Wecin, ;i11:3;-; ;while lying off Stonehave Harbour, a squall threv. on her beamends the Stonehaven schooner Benmo re,coa 1-laden from Sunderland. It was impossible to right the vessel, which went down. The skipper and another man were drowned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Most Nutritious.</head>
        <p>The Most Nutritious.</p>
        <p>EPPS'S</p>
        <p>Grateful—Comiorting.</p>
        <p>C C• A</p>
        <p>Breakfastr—Supper.</p>
        <p>THE P.S.A. MOVEMENT. Tenth Anniversary of the Wycliff 3 Society.</p>
        <p>Councillor nnett and Education.</p>
        <p>The Rev. JOHN YONGE presided at the tenth anniver- sary of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society, held on Sunday afternoon. Thera was an encouraging attendance and the workers were kept busy enrolling members for the eleventh year. _ _</p>
        <p>The Rev. JOHN YONGE thought they might begin by mutually congratulating themselves that they had that day reached their tenth birthday as a i'.S.A. Society. Some of them remembered that when upon the first Sunday in March, 1893, the society was inaugurated there were kindly prophets who ventured upon the pre- diction that about six months would see it through. He would venture to say that the society would be in existence for some time longer. (Hear, hear.) He was perfectly certain from what he knew from private information and from what ethers had told him that during the past ten years there had been a great many men who thanked God thatthey ever came within those walls and attended the P.S.A. meetings.. Men has been raised up from a .condition into which they had fallen; they had come to a knowledge of the faith and love of Jesus Christ, and become better men, better fathers, better citizens hind more able to take their share in the work and Tesponsibilities of life. Many Sunday afternoon gatherings had been started fo man since the P.S.A. commenced in Warrington. They at Wycliffe did not complain. There was plenty of work for all. He had noticed many young fellows strolling about on Sunday afternoon apparently with nothing to do except smoke cigarettes, put their hands in their pockets, stand at street corners, and carry on rather hilarious conversation. These men were proper sub- jects for recruits to the P.S.A. Society and if the mem- bers would give tberaselves to unselfish service they could be brought in and the membership instead of be- ing 570 would soon be double. (Hear, hear.) Councillor BENsarr delivered an address and said the members might be legitimately proud of the tea years' work of the P.S.A. Society in Warrington. He wished them to consider the broad general principles which underlay education. The object of education,he said was to bring out the best possibilities of our manhood, and to help each one of them to reach the full stature of a man. In discussing what was necessary to attain this ideal manhood, Councillor Bennett said first of all we must give more attention to physical development. He meant something very different, from coursing at Waterloo or yelling at Wilderspool Causeway. He meant the development of oar physical bodies, and this should begin in the schools. A good deal was being done. in this direction already. It ought to be an essential part of our education that the children should be trained in the gymnasia of this country and developed on scientific lines. More use should be made of the baths sal every boy and girl ought to be taught to swim. By following these lines we might do something to bring out the best in our physical manhood. Certainly- they would do a good deal more than by simply watch- ing other people play games and perhaps laying some odds on the result. We ought to familiarise ourselves with the old ideals of physical beauty and try to attain that physical perfection which was gained by the Greeks° Councillor Bennett proceeded to point out that the culture Of the mind was even more important than culture of the body. Whatever occupation our children might have to fulfil they were all the better for being thoroughly educated. Technical education was highly necessary if we were to maintain our manu- facturing supremacy and keep our place in the forefront of the battle of life, but after all there was a danger in technical education if it was to be looked upon as merely a sort of bread and butter knowledge. If we only ac- quired knowledge for the sake of earning a livelihood then we did not understand the real value of knowledge. Referring to the necessity of an interest being taken in political and municipal affairs, Councillor Bennett asked how many took what might be called an intelligent interest in municipal affairs and really tried to get to the roots of things. Possibly if more did so they would judge those who served them a little more charitably How many tried to study the essential principles in politics. Many of them followed like sheep the party leaders, yet upon our politics the very welfare and life of the nation depended, and if there was one thing we should take an interest in it was in matters politicaland municipal. As an illustration of that how many had read the history of our own town, or our country or knew anything about our world-wild empire beyond what they read occasionally in an evening paper? He urged his hearers to study something deeper and better than the ephemeral literature of the day. That P.S.A. Society encouraged them to read good books by means of the prize scheme, and he could assure them that the happiest, pleasantest and profitablest hours of his life I had been spent in the company of good books. Education, he proceeded to state, began in the cradle, and it ought not to end in the school. It should go on through life. He wanted them to aim at being strong, beautiful and wise. They could not get all the pleasure out of lifaunless they endeavoured to be wise by culti- vating their mental faculties, by reading and observing and especially by thinking. In conclusion he referred to Christ as the perfect man, and said in our schools and churches and in our lives the one object should be to try to attain the full stature of man in Christ Jesus, for that was after all the only true manhood. (Ap- plause.) The PitEsiDENTannounced that the new roll of mem- bers for the eleventh year which had been opened that day contained 398 names already. The membership last year was 572, and upwards of 9,000 books had been received by the members. The sick club was still doing valuable work with a membership of nearly 100, and the holiday savings club started last year had already over 100 depositors, whose weekly contributions amounted ' to between £7 and £B. The money would be distributed in Walking Day week. He urged the members to do Itheir utmost to enlarge the membership of the society. Solos were contributed by Mr. W. H. Wallington. Mr. W. H. Payton presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>DOES PARALYSIS THREATEN? Dr. Williams' Pink Pills Care.</head>
        <p>DOES PARALYSIS THREATEN? Dr. Williams' Pink Pills Care.</p>
        <p>Paralysis threatens people who are not aware of it. Do you realise the horrors of it—the ever-growing pain the utter inabili y to move the palsied limbs, the torturing needle-pains in the spine, Death by inches creeping up the body? These things do not all appear at once. You may be in danger without knowing it. Numbness and loss of sensation in eitherarm or leg is not by any means the first sign. If your back feels weak and lame, if you have a dull pain across the bottom of the spine, if your hand is sometimes very unsteady, and (in man) manly strength diminished, there is a great likelihood that Paralysis threatens. In one form—the worst—loss of balance is a sign; you cannot stand upright, having the eyes shut, without, after a second or two, beginning to sway about. This is a sign of Locomotor Ataxy; the torture in which men and women die who have this disease is too horrible to be described. Worst of all, it was long believed to be incurable. People pronounced ABSOLUTELY INC URABLE</p>
        <p>after examination by the most learned doctors have, however, been cured during the last few years by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. Naturally, it is well to take them before the disease has had time to develop too far. At the first signs they should be taken They have warded off many an attack, saved many a man from a life of agony, many a family from starvation through the slow death of its Bread-winner. Mr. Thompson, New House Farm, Caldwell, Darling- ton, was told by all the doctors who examined him that he had no chance of recovery. His face was contorted by paralysis. At first he only felt a numbness coming over him. But if he closed his eyes he staggered and fell, and after a time he was seized by tearing pains in the back. A sensation, as if a cord were tied tight round his middle, was another thing he complained of. He was suffering, in short, from Locomotor Ataxy, the most terrible of all paralysis. When all doctors gave him up, Dr. Williams' Pink Kris were tried. Their effect soon convinced everyone that they could cope with the disease. Mr. Thompson when last seen had so far recovered that, with the exception of a little bit of shakiness in his hands. he is nearlywell. At one time he had been so bad that he could not feed himself. _ _</p>
        <p>He now walked unaided even by a stick, and said that he was quite amazed at the cure which had been wrought after he had quite given up hope. The minor ailments of life naturally yield more easily than these severe cases. Rheumatism, Anwmia, St. Vitus' Dance, the ailments troublesome to women. Headache, Backache, and Kidney Disease are among the disorders which Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People (but only the genuine pills, which may be distinguished by bearing Dr. Williams' name) have cured. Six boxes 13s. 9d.—one box 2s. 9d. At all medicine shops, or post free from Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, Holborn- viaduct, London. The pills are not relaxing or purga- tive : on the contrary, they strengthen. •</p>
        <p>At an inquest at Stockton on Tuesday on Arthur Dobson, who drowned himself in the River Tees on Monday night, it was stated that he was of weok intel- lect, and had not wiorked for some years. Since Friday night he had been much concerned because he had had a dream about drowning himself. A verdict of" Suicide while temporarily insane "was returned. Katherina Duchess of Westminster, and her daughter, • Lady Mory 'Grosvenor, left theis Cheshire residence • Combermere Abbey, near Whitchurch, on Tuesday, for their London residence, 44, Grosvenor Square, for the season. They were accompanied by Viscount Crichton, • who had heen spending a few days at the Abbey and hvnting with the Cheslitae, North Stafford, and Sir Watkin Wynn's hounds. The Bill introduced in the House of Commons on Tuesday evening by Sir Howard Vincent proposes to make it a misdemeanour for an already convicted alien to come into this country, and to provide also ehat in the case of any alien convicted of crime in this country , deportation shall form part of his sentence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOW: COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOW: COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Al UNEVENTFUL SITTING,</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrinatm Town Council was held on Tuesday evening, the business being trans- acted in the unusually short time of 35 minutes. The Mayor (Mr. J. C. Parr) presidad, and there were also present : —The Deputy-Mayor (Alderman J. Fairclough), Aldermon W. Bolton, J. S. Green, T. Hutchinson, F. W. Monks, J. Smethurst, T. H: Sutton; Councillors, T. Barton, W. Brough, J. Brophy, I'. Burton, J. G. Craik, S. Dzvies. .1. Dodds, D. S. Duncan, James Evans, John Eva.ns, L. Ellison, T. J. Hulse, J. Lord, A, Peacock, J. Pemberton, W. Pierpoint, Wu. Roberts, W. Rogers, J. Sampson. D. Tinnion, J. Upson,J. %Wilkinson ;Messrs. J. L.Whittle,(town clerk);S. Wilson,(deputyitown clerk); J. Fairhurst, (borough treasurer); J. Dens, (water engineer); W. S. Haddock, (gas engineer); J. G. Gomall, (medical officer); J. Al. Murray, (director of education. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>LATE MR: MUIR,</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR: MUIR,</p>
        <p>The MAYOR reported the death of Mr. Muir, a former member of the Town Connell, and moved the usual vote of condolence with relatives. His Worship said he had not had the advantage of Mr. Alair's personal acquaint- ance, but he was a member of the Council some years ago,</p>
        <p>--The DEPUTY MAYOR seconded, and the motion was carried.</p>
        <p>THE AUDIT6RS,</p>
        <p>! The MAYOR formally minounced that Messrs. Law- rence Bolton and Edwin Bradshaw had been elected the elective auditors of the borough for the ensuing year. He had not yet made the appointment of the Mayor's auditor.</p>
        <p>WATCH COMMITTEE. I At a meeting _held on October 3rd it was resolved that the tender of Messrs. James, Smith and Company Limited, of Derby, to supply police clothing according to specification, at £289 75., be accepted, also the tender of Messrs. Unsworth Bros., to supply helmets, caps, gloves &amp;c., according to specification, at £l4 10s. 6d. L icences were granted to 55 drivers of hackney carriages. —On the motion of the MAYOR, seconded by Alderman S t7r roN. the minutes were confirmed.</p>
        <p>QUEEN'S HOTEL.-THP4 OVERSEERS.</p>
        <p>The Finance and Est-tte Committee, at mectin,gs held on February 9th and February 23rd, passed the following resolutions :—That the tender of Mr. John Rigby to rent the Queen's Hotel, Sankey-street, for a term of three years from the 25th March next, the tenancy thereafter to be determinable on six months' notice from any date, be accepted, and that Messrs. Joseoh Shaw Green, Arthur Bennett, John Wilkinson,and James Grice Craik, be'appoiated overseers of the poor of the towgiship of Warrington for the ensuing year. Alderman. Shaw Green proposed the confirmation of the minutes. Councillor Wilkinson seconded, and the motion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>COST OF STREET LIGHTING</head>
        <p>COST OF STREET LIGHTING</p>
        <p>At a melting of the Street Improvement and Light- ing Cornmitte3 held on the 19th February it. was re- solved that the Gas Eaginear be requested to make a joint report with the Borough Surveyor and Electrical Engineer on the comparative cost of street lighting.. These minutes were agreed to on the motion of Alder- man Sutton, seconded by Councillor Barton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>PAVING AND SEWERAGE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>PAVING AND SEWERAGE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>• The minutes related principally to the acceptance of tenders, and a letter was read intimating that the Post- master-General, acting under the powers conferred upon him by the Telegraphs Acts, 1883 to 1892, was desirous of taking up the footpath pavement on the north side of Sankey-street, east side of Crosfield-street, north side of Proghall-lane and Tanners-lane, and east side of Winwick-road, for the purpotse of laying a .three-inch iron pipe.—lt was resolved that the application be granted on the usual terms. _</p>
        <p>Councillor TINS lON proposed the confirmation of the minutes, and said the committee wished to withdraw the resolution with reference to the proposed paving with wood setts of the roadway on the north side of the Sacred Heart Church in Liverpool-road. He said the committee had reconsidered ,the matter in conse- quence of a representation made to them, and they had thought it desirable to pave the whole width of the street. In the first place the committee did not think it desirable to pave the whole width, but on consider- ing the small cost(about £7O) and the influence brought to bear upon them on the matter, they thought that if the whole width was paved with wood setts it would make a better job of it. He referred to the taking up of footpaths mentioned in the minutes, and said it was a great pity, but the committee had no option. Alderman HuTc HINSON See onded.</p>
        <p>Counoillor HULSE called attention to the state of Gorsey-lane, about which he had been approached by the residents. He asked for information with refer- ence to the passage between Fairfield House and Daintith's property,and wished to know if any progress had been made. Councillor ROGERA remarked that the matter was considered the previous night and the committee had proraised.to givc their attention to Gorsey-lane. . .</p>
        <p>Councillor-Tfss[oN observed that CoUncillor Craik did a very sensible and commendable thing the previous night in reference to some other streets. He went to the committee and laid the matter fully before them. That course gave them a much better idea of what was necessary. It would be better if councillors would come in that way to the committee and give notice of any questions they might ask. Councillor Hulse was rather out of order and his remark was unnecessary. So far as the Paving and Sewerage Committee were con- cerned they were willing at all times to receive such help and advice, because there were many wards which various councillors knew more about than the committee did. He endeavoured to look after his own ward, and it would help the committee very much if councillors would like Councillor Craik, approach them on such matters.</p>
        <p>Councillor HULSE said he had approached both the officials and the committee butno notice had been taken of the matter.</p>
        <p>The minutes were adopted. THE TOWN HALL FLAG. Alderman BOLTON proposed that the minutes of the Town Hall, etc., Committees be confirmed. Alderman Sm ET II I_7 rt sT seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor DODDS : I think we might have a new flag for:the Town Hall. (Laughter.) The present one is in a most dilapidated condition. Alderman Bouros remarked that they would put that and everything else in order when they had got to know the amount of damage the gale had done.</p>
        <p>The minutes were THE UNDERGROUND CONVENEENCES.—THE ATTENDANTS' WAGES.—WORSE THAN SWEATING.</p>
        <p>None of the business of the Markets Committee con- tained any feature of public interest. Alderman HUTCHINSON moved, and Councillor RoGERs seconded, the adoption of the minutes. Councillor PIERPO INT, alluding to the minute with reference to the undetground conveniences in the market, said he thought the Chairman of the Committee would have given the Council some information. He saw that they proposed to give a male attendant 14s. a week, and a female attendant us. a week. They would have to work 66 hours per week, and that worked out to 2.54 pence per hour for the man and PB3 pence for the female. He failed to see what kind of attendants the Committee expected for such a valuable position—(laughter)—which could not be called" sweating "—it was not a half bad enough term to apply. When they asked a woman—and she must be a real woman—to work 66 hours at 1.83 pence per hour, it was a very serious thing for that Council. Alderman HUTCHINSON, in reply, said the idea was quite a new one. It was not intended to putable bodied people in the positions, but someone who could not accept any other kind of employment. In other places the slot system was used, but it had not proved success- ful, and in having attendants they were starting a new business. If it was a success the amount of wages would be progressive. They considered that the amount named was a fair one to start with, and if they found they could pay more they would do so.</p>
        <p>the mina(); were then agreed to. THE WIDENING OF WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>QUESTION AGAIN TO BE SFIORTLY CONSIDERED..</p>
        <p>At-:a meeting of the Gas Committee it was resolved that Joseph Grounds and Ughtred 0. Burrell be ap- pointed meter inspectors at 22s.per wnek,with uni forms: that the Engineer and Deputy Town Clerk attend a con- ference of local authorities, to be held atCrewe, for the purpose of arranging combined opposition to the North-Western Electricity and Power Gas Bill, 1903 ; that the General Purposes Committee be requested to petition against the North-Western Electricity and Power Gas Bill, and to join in the combined opposition of the various local authorities and gas companies affected by the Bill ; and that the plan submitted by the engineer of proposed new main across the east side of Warrington Bridge be approved. Alderman FAIRCLOUGH, in moving the adoption of the minutes, remarked that instead of taking the foot- path and roadway over Warrington Bridge up, they were going to put the main along the outside of the bridge on the east side. (Hear, hear). Alderman BOLTOX seconded.</p>
        <p>Alderman SSIVHURST said he hoped the committee would take into consideration that before long they would have a meeting of the committee formed in re- ference to the widening of the bridge, and that they should not do anything in the way of obstruction. They were only waiting until the trams had been running about six months to see how they went on, and how also the traffic went on, and he would certainly then propose that the committee meet and at once go into the ques- tion of widening or making some alteration. He hoped the main would not be placed so as to prevent canti- levers being used. In London cantilevers 20ft. wide were being erected, and he did not see why they could not have them in Warrington. They would cost .£3,000 instead of .£30,000 as had been proposed to be spent on the bridge. Alderman FAIRCLOUGH said what they were going to do was a matter of necessity, and they could not wait for the widening of the bridge. The people in Cheshire</p>
        <p>wanted gas, and if it was later on decided to put canti- levers at the bridge they would have to take up the main and put it down again. 1 The minutes were agreed to. I THE PUMPING SPATION AT DELPH-LANE.</p>
        <p>Ceunciller Joas EVANS, in proposing the adoption of the minutes of the Water Committee, observed that no doubt the Council would be pleased to hear that the pumping engines at Delph-lane were near completion and it was hoped to have a trial run in the course of a few days. Tao motion was agreed to. GYMNASIUI COMM ITT EE.—RESIGNATION OF Alit. VERNON.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. N. VERNON having tendered his resignation as hon. secretary to the committee it was resolved that, whileaccepting the resignation with much regret, the committee desire to place on record their appreciation of the valuable services rendered by Mr. Vernon to the institution, extending over a period of ten years. Councillor TINNION proposed the confirmation of the Gymnasium Committee. He said Mr. Vernon had been secretary for ten years, and as an athlete himself he had taken considerable interest in the institution and done as much work 113 most individual members of the com- mittee. The committee wished to show their full appreciation of his services during the past ten years. • Alderman SMETHURST seconded and remarked that Mr. Vernon had been a very good secretary. The com- mittee was losing a worthy servant. The minutes were confirmed. TRANSPORT OF MERCHANDISE.</p>
        <p>Alderman MONKS proposed the adoption of the minutes of the Electricity and Tramways Committee. The minutes contained a resolution that the tender of Mr. Thomas Daintith, to supply two Humber bicycles at £23 10s. be accepted. The following letter was also read :—Lancashire Transport of Merchandise Committee, Municipal Buildings, Liverpool, W., 21st January, 1903. Dear sir,-1 have been instructed by the committee appointed at the meeting held on the 19th December last to forward to you a copy of the enclosed report of the General Manager of the Liverpool Tramways Under- taking, explaining in outline the general nature of the scheme, together with a copy of the plan referred to in the report. The coainittee will be glad to hear, not later than the 21st proximo, whether your Council are prepared to give facilities within their district for enabling the scheme to be carried out.—Yours ERNEsT W. PIERCE, Hon. Sec. The Town Clerk, Warrington. It was resolved that the Town Clerk be instructed to reply to the letter, stating that the committee are pre- pared to favourably consider any proposals the Lanca- shire Transport of _Merchandise Committee may sill:knit. THE PROPOSED IMPROVEMENT AT ARPLEY</p>
        <p>ROAD. THE WARRINGTON SLATE COMPANY WITHDRAWS FROM THE ARRANGEMENT.</p>
        <p>The General Purposes Committee recommended that a petition be presented to Parliament against the North- Western Electricity and Power Gas Bill, and that the Corporation oppose the Bill on the terms proposed by the Gas Committee; that the Town Clerk and the Borough Treasurer be requested to attend thrsConfer- ence on the problem of unemployment, to beheld in the Guildhall, London, on the 27th and 28th inst. That the Mayor and Town Clerk be appointed to attend the annual meeting of the Association of Municipal Cor- porations, to be held in London on the 21st March next; that the Mayor, Town Clerk, Borough Member, and the Parliamentary Agent be invited to attend the annual dinner of the A.M.C. as the guests of the Corpora- tion,</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk submitted the draft agreement be- tween Lord Lilford and the Corporation for the pur- chase of the recreation ground at Little Sankey, which was approved. It was resolved that the Open Spaces Sub-Committee be requested to make arrangements for a formal opening of the recreation ground when the purchase has been completed. The Committee also resolved that the Board of Education be requested to amend the scheme constitut- ing an education committee for the Borough by increas- ing tha number of persons to be recommended by an association of Roman Catholic elementary schools to two, and alliwing one member to be recommended by the Warrincon Friendly Societies. It was further resolved that the No-Municipal Trad- ing Committee b 3 requested to appoint three represen- tatives to confer with three members of the Town oouncil with the object of d3vising the form and extent of aa inquiry into the alleged illegal and immoral trad- ing who will be acceptahleto both parties. Another recommendation of the Committee was that the common seal of the Corporation be affixed to an agreement with the London and North-Western Railway Company re Arp I ey- read. The Nf.a.voit moved the confirm'ktion of the minutes. The Dsetyrv-Mxvo!t seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD said before the Council voted upon these minutes he would like to as a question with reference to tho proposed agreement with the London and North-Western Railwa.y Compiny regarding a pro- posed improVement at the corner of A7rpley-road and Wilson-Patten-street. He said h believed there was a clause in the agree neat wit!' reference to the purchase of some property belonging to the Slate Company Limited—property which would have to be removed by reason of the proposed improvement—and the price for which was to be fixed by Mr. William Owen. Gould the Mayor tell him if the amotuit had been fixbd. The MAYOR: I ain.illormed it has not.</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD: Will it bein order pass this deed without knowing watt we aro going to pay for 0., property? • • The MAYOu said they had received a letter from the Slate CompanY,which the Town Clerk would read. Toe TOWN CLERK then re td the following :—" March 3rd, 1903. Dear Mr. Mayor,—We are informed that notice has been given to rescind thearrangement entered into between the Corporation and ourselves some time ago for the purpose of carrying out a much-needed im- prove:neat at the corner of Wilson-Patten-street and Arpley-roia We understand perfectly the nature of the opposition, and we think the public of Warrington will be able teapdreciate it at its true value. At the same time, while we know that the improvement is necessary for the safety and protection of the public and that it iminnot be carried out without acquiring a small property belonging to us; and further, although we were quite prepared to have taken the valuation of either the gentleman selected by the Council or of any other com- petent person for the property in question, we cannot consent to have this matter re-opened. We, therefore beg respectfully to withdraw entirely from the arrange- meat made.—Yours respectfully, for the Warrington Slate Company, Limited, H. G. ROBERTS." Councillor LORD: Are we to understand that the whole matter is dropped? The MAYOR: SO I understand.</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD: The agreement is to be with- drawn ?</p>
        <p>The TOWN CLERK: Yes.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : Does the agreement be- • taPeen the Railway Company and this Corporation lapse? The TowNCLERK : A part of tile agreement will be carried out with the Railway Company. The MAYOR: 'That part of the agreement relating to this particular property of the Slate Company will fall through. Councillor LORD: I object to this matter going through at the present time. I have given notice of my intention to move that the resolution of the Council authorising the agreement be rescinded. lam not in order in moving that motion now but it affects this agreement which is now proposed. Councillor PEMBERTON: I move that the General Purposes Committee's minutes be confirmed with the exception of the one relating to the agreement. Alderman SUTTON: There is no necessity to move an amendment. Th 4 agreement is withdrawn. Councillor WILKINSON: I should like to ask who has withdrawn it? The Warrington Slate Company has nothing to do with our agreement with the London and North Western Railway Company with reference to improving this road. The TOWN CLERK: The withdrawal of the Slate Company from the arrangement will necessitate an alteration of the plans. Councillor WILKINSON: 'cannot see 'where the Slate Company come in. Mr. William Owen was instructed to make a valuation of the Slate Company's old office buil I suppose he has not made that valuation? The MAYOR: The Slate Company withdraw the offer of the proiierty. We are not withdrawing from our agreement with the Londonand North Western Railway Company. The Tows CLERK: An alteration of the plans will have to be made.</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD: Will the matter of the agreement with the Railway Company come before the General Purposes Committee or the Council? The MAYOR: That matter will be brought up again. THE NEW EDUCATION ACT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>QUESTION OF DISQUALIFICATION.</head>
        <p>THE QUESTION OF DISQUALIFICATION.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR moved that the disqualification of any persons who are Members of the Council, and who will become disqualified from holding office in consequence of the Education Act, 1902 shall not take effect until the Ist day of November, 1903. His Worship explained that it was a formal motion in order to remove a dis- qualification which was affecting the convenience of the Council and of certain people. The DEPUTY IVINYOR seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor W. ROBERTS: I should like to ask when the new education authority will cone into operation. The TOWN CLERK: On the Ist April. (Laughter). Alderman MONKS: Does it not depend upon the Board of Education expressing their approval? The TOWN CLERK: They have expressed their opinion already. Councillor WILLIAM ROBERTS: When will the re- presentatives of the committee be appointed by the Council?</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: At a special meeting called for that purpose. I don't think it has yet been decided when it will be held.</p>
        <p>Councillor THCNION: May I ask the Town Clerk who is the authority for electing the third of the foundation managers. Will it be the new Education Committee or the Council?</p>
        <p>The TOWN CLERK: It all depends. If you delegate the duty to the Education Committee they will make the appointment. •</p>
        <p>Councillor JAMES EVANS : I understandfrom an ob- servation just made that the Council has that .power within itself.</p>
        <p>The TOWN CLERK: Certainly. Councillor JAMES EVANS : And that will remain so until it relegates its power to the new Education Com- mittee?</p>
        <p>The TOWN CLERK: Certainly. The motion was then carried. COUNCILLOR LORD'S MOTION. THE ROAD AT ARPLEY STATION.</p>
        <p>TheMAYoa said the only other business before the Council was the motion standing in Councillor Lord's name.</p>
        <p>The motion was as follows :—" That the resolution passed by the Council on Tuesday, February sth, 1901, that the terms of the letter from the London and North-Western Railway Company relating to the road at Arpley Station be agreed to, be rescinded. That the resolution passed at the Council meeting on Tuesday, February sth, 1901, that Mr. William Owen fix a price to be paid to the Warrington Slate Company, Limited for the old office, which will have to be removed by, reason of the improvement, be rescinded."</p>
        <p>The MAYOR silted what' Councillor Lord intended to do.</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD: What are we to understand. Is the matter coming forward again, or shall I go on with it ?</p>
        <p>The iNIAYOR : It is open for you to go on with the resolution if you like.</p>
        <p>Alderman SurroN : It is not necessary to rescind the resolution. That has already been done. Councillor TINNION : The agreement has been with- drawn.</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON : I take it that what we shall have to do will be to reconsider the whole question and bring it forward again. (Hear, hear.) Councillor LORD: I am quite agreeable to that personally. There are other objections to the improve- ment, beside the Slate Company's office. The MA.YOR : Do I understand that you withdraw the resolution for the present? Councillor LORD: If the matter will come forward again. _ _</p>
        <p>-The MAYOR : It will have to come forward again. That assurance has been already given. The resolution was then withdrawn, and the Council rose.</p>
        <p>How a Mother of Sturdy Miners Was Cured.</p>
        <p>She had indigestion and Biliousness for 40 Years, but Bile Beans Made Her Well.</p>
        <p>The eyes of the reading public were but recently turned to the strike doings and the evictions in the South Yorkshire coalfield, one incident of which—a scene at aneviction is sketched above. From Mexboro' where the full force of the strike was felt, comes a sensational story of another great triumph by that world-famed medicine, Chas Fordo's Bile Beans. The facts :we given below:—</p>
        <p>In view of the above sworn statement the great value of Bile Beans as a family medicine cannot be disputed. Every sufferer from indigestion, anemia, debility, con- stipation, piles, loss of appetite, pimples, and blood impurities is hereby invited to forward this coupon to the Bean Bile Co.'s Cen- tral Depot, Leeds, withl FREE SAMPLE. I</p>
