      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>The shops look gay and attractive at this season, especially as the goods displayed cora- bine the rich or diaphanous fabrics for evening wear with the delicately tinted materials for spring costumes. There is a marked tendency towards pale colours of all sorts, but unless one nossesses a particularly well-stocked wardrobe it will be found best to select something which will not readily soil. Pastel tones have still a certain vogue, but one would soon tire of any costume of a bright though soft shade. Grey is always a popular colour, and is generally becoming, while it has the advantage of har- monizing with any coloured hat one may elect to wear in conjunction with it, and the floral toques this season are particularly gay. There is, however, something very charming in the massing together of small blossoms such as roses, violets, or forget-me-note into the deep- brimmed flat-crowned toques so fashionable. A narrow chaplet of leaves is sometimes laid on round the centre of the upturned brim. Large picturesque hats of straw in pale blue, rose, or violet shades look very pretty with only a drapery of lace surrounding the low crown</p>
        <p>which is composed of flowers. Sometimes narrow black velvet ribbon is substituted for the lace. There are also some hats suited for dressy occasions made of white tulle illusion trimmed with sweeping paradise tails. The pretty hat depicted is of this mode with a few black lace motifs to relieve the dead white effect. A strong contrast to the chic simplicity of this millinery confection was a bright red toque made of geraniums and a few of their leaves, which in spite of the strong colour looked well in conjunction with a black tailor- made gown! All hats are conspicuous by the flatness of their crowns and the width of their brims, while the toque proper has also a flat but wide crown and a rather deep brim. Bright emerald green is a colour much admired this season, and an exceedingly smart toque in this shade was made entirely of closely curled ostrich feathers with a small military aigrette at the side. Of course, such a piece of millinery was somewhat costly, but very nearly the same effect can be got by green tulle closely drawn and gathered. A very pretty floral trimming which is quite one of this season's novelties is tile fuchsia in the various tones of colouring in which the flower naturally appears. There is a grace about the drooping blossoms which is undoubted and harmonizes well with curving outlines of a broad brimmed hat of picturesque design. There should certainly be no difficulty this season in selecting a becoming hat, for there never were so many styles to choose from.</p>
