      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>NO% ELTY.</head>
        <p>A NO% ELTY.</p>
        <p>/Another charming novelty (which has found favour already, and will cootinue to do so as the season- advances) is the lace bolero, a charming specimen of which is given in my illustration. There are many varieties of this pretty little article of dress, some being ex- pressed in substantial materials such as silk or satin, while others again are of fine lace or drawn chiffon int, rutingled with lace applique. A strong make of lace constitutes the body part of the coatee depleted while the hard effect is softened by soft full frills of a handsomely edged lace with a fine net ground. The colour is biscuit or a dull cream colour. A bolero or coatee of this s!ylo is su7h a useful addition to one's outfi,, as it can be quickly drawn on over a plain silk blouse or will go equally well with an elaborwe demi-toileite. There are some very pP etty bolero, of very tine lace with the sleeve falling away from the elbow in a deep frill of lace to march, while o! hers apin are adorned with sequins of an opal.--cent description. One very smart coatee trimmed in this fashion was</p>
