      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>SPIIING TOILETTE.</head>
        <p>A SPIIING TOILETTE.</p>
        <p>worn with a flat-crowned toque of drawn cream chiffon, the deep brim being covered with these mother o' pearl glistening sequins. A sweeping paradise plume waved from one side, the toilette owning its existence to the onderful taste of one of the controllers of the sartorial world.</p>
        <p>The toilette depicted i of the most up-to-date description. It is made with one of the detach- able caps so much in vogue at present, and also boasts a skirt of the smartest description. The skirt is knife-pleated up to the waist, the pleats narrowing off in the most wonderful manner so as to avoid giving the figure a bulky appearance. If the wearer had been at all in- clined to embonpoint while evincing a decided intention to adopt this particular style of skirt the pleats would not have been brought to the waist line but would have finished in a shaped band or empiecement. The little coat is short enough to show the deep shaped band of silk, and by the by let me point out what a chan has come over our waist belts this. season. In- stead of narrowing down in front to the smallest possible width the belt now is wide all round the waist and requires considerable skill in the making thereof to achieve success, for it must fit the figure accurately. The belt is held in place by wide slides at the sides, centre bpolc and front, the latter being generally of an ornamental description. These belts are usually of a soft make of silk,</p>
        <p>COQUETTL</p>
      </div>
