      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Next Tuesday, March 10th, will be the fortieth versary of their Majesties' wedding day. Everybody,lo familiar with the fact that. the twenty-fifth year is silver wedding, the fiftieth the golden, and the sis.tle4; the diamond, but though no similar term has bee popularly applied to the fortieth anniversary, yet completion of four decades of married life by the A. and Queen suggest many thoughts of more than pass'l'' interest. There have been many joys and sorrows der ing those long years, but from the first when Qu'"), Alexandra landed at Gravesend on the 6th of Mere" 1863 and made her triumphal progress through Load0o? she has been regarded with love and esteem by tile whole nation. The wedding took place four days later in St. Geerge:° Chapel, Windsor, in the presence of the then receeti widowed Queen Victoria' and the honeymoon was sPeD,,t at Osborne. The King, then Prince of Wales Wes that time in his 22nd year, and Princess AlexandreL her nineteenth, but though forty years have passed tn", Queen is happy in the circumstance that her 00_1 principal supporters at her wedding, namely, her father, , the King of Denmark, and the Duke of Cambridge) °'re still amongst the living. Princes and Princesses are usually married le Royal Palaces, and occasionally there is a great mentary wedding at Westminster, but there is nosc. be a similar interesting event at the Mansion House London, where Miss Samuel, the daughter of the 14.0 L. Mayor and Lady Mayoress, is to be married to Walter Levy. This ceremony will be performed by hs.h Chief Rabbi on April 7th, and it will be the first marriage to take place at the official residence of TP, Chief Magistrate of the City. The ceremony °f f o Hebrew wedding has many picturesque features, el which will of course be seen at their best on this oar sion. s 5 The annual sale of Irish industries will be be--,,A0 usual on St. Patrick's Day, when it is hoped the Ong will pay a visit to the exhibition. This however 4 uncertain as her Majesty will be leaving for Denint'"ci during the third week in March, but in any event 10 members of the Royal Family are sure to attend Irish sale. Last year it was held at Lord Salisb,V,t house in Arlington-street, this year it will be hew - Lord Londonderry's mansion in Park-lane. This is not so large as Lansdowne House, but all threo reception rooms will be thrown open, so that vlBlt°,4 will have an opportunity of seeing one of the b'er houses in London. The display of goods such as bora spans, knittings, lace, needlework, art ware,and furnitur.et will, it is said be better than usual,l and many sof Oa; leading women in Ireland will be amongst the.. .stt holders, including the Duchess of Abercorn, Lady downe, Lady Cadogen, Lady Londonderry, Lady P"-- cannon, Lady Mayo, and several others. • The lack of cupboards is a common complaint .1 newly built houses, and the King and Queen were quie,,, to perceive the same defect in the London Coue'_•,,i Council's industrial dwellings, which their Majest'e; recently visited at Millbank. Cupboards may be abusel and those people who use them only to hide away litteo and rubbish, had best be without them, but for tbo .f who live in two or three rooms, they are a necessitY,ll the place is to be kept tidy. No doubt more regard Wi, be paid to these requirements in tenement dwelling 7 now that the Queen has drawn attention to them. middle class houses, it is perhaps not to be regrett7 that the builders have largely given up the practioo:o fixing cupboards, as with the jerry-built walls they nearly always damp. The old-fashioned cupboards, I.r which our grandmothers gloried to store away theli possessions were really only possible in the older all(' more solidly-built houses. Little bolero coats to match the skirts with which they are worn, promise to enjoy considerable favour this spring and summer, but the blouse instead of be:, ing in direct contrast as regards colour, is this seascf to match the bolero and skirt exactly. All the 11°`: blouses show much ornamentation' in the shape of filo7, embroideries worked in the most dainty colourings .A 0 aginable, and the woollen laces which have had a vogue during the past winter, show no signs of vanl,s4 ing. On the contrary they are adorning many of new spring toilettes, and modistes predict a fresh leP of popularity for them, when the linen gowns apPear',,e A new variety consists of little balls, made of t"„ same materials as the gown, and in shape resemblie,b_ acorn cups, mounted on more or less elaborate Prilg tures of silk cord passementerie. For dainty eveal4 gowns the little balls are carried out in chiffon, muslin, or soft satin ribbon. By the ,way, those ael°"o us who have a knowledge of ribbon workshould lose Pes time in copying some of the beautiful floral garnitiq composed entirely of satin ribbons, in various widtbs; pleated and gathered .up into the close resemblance ~ buds, blossoms and foliage. For the trimming of evello big cloaks and coats, and evening gowns, they are . . .</p>
        <p>great request just now. bag Sleeves are becoming more modified. The huge .4 falling from the elbow to the wrist which mo have for some time past deemed correct, is grovill smaller, and we can but trust the process will contielle until this grotesque, and vory inconvenient appendage vanishes altogether. Small bishop sleeves have appearr:,, in many of the new spring gowns, and these are bot'' pretty and comfortable. There appears to be every scope for the suggestil that establishments should be started where we co% put out our mending, the same as we put out our Nos" ing. No one now has any time for patching and daillj lug. In spite of labour saving appliances, househ°,,a duties, and the family needs seem ever to multiply, 3-,0 it is a rare thing to find a servant who knows how, if she were willing, to mend and darn, as in the 01a,es4 days the housewife used to do with patience, neato"A and skill. Many people would be only too glad to Pe"is out their mending, and in these days of bureaus, wb/,:i; make a speciality of a certain class of household 10',1! and will even send in a staff of servants to do the „ie-0 tire spring cleaning in two or three days, it ser",e(l strange that there are, as yet no properly orgailL'er establishments, for_the repair of personal and hew hold linen, and wearing apparel. prs The doctors are agreed that the number of suffer,;et, from amemia is steadily increasing, especially amenbo growing girls, and the cause of this state of affairs.,,ja, nearly always deficient nutrition. It is quite possivto to have as much food as one can eat, and yet net properly nourished, and consequently the wasting Pre, cess goes on. To all such,Virol, which is a dietetispro pamtion of Red Bone Marrow, Malt Extract, Vre ig Eggs, with the lime of their shells, and Lemon juicrla of incalculable value, end not only to the armmic. .:;"0 cases of delicate children, consumption, and all wastoT eft',,diseases it has invariably had the most beneficial eft,, unlike cod liver oil, it is pleasant to take. It csa be spread upon toast or biscuit, or added to bread all milk, porridge, cornflour, or arrowroot.</p>
      </div>
