        <p>THE " SANITAS " COMPANY LIMITED.—The annual general meeting of this company was held on Wednes- day, 25th February, 1903, the Chairman, Mr. C. T. Kin,v- zett, F.1.C., F.C.S.,,presiding. On behalf of the Board be congratulated the shareholders -upon the continued prosperity of the business, and said that although the weather experienced during the last spring and sunimer was altogether unfavourable to its welfare, the sales and revenue of the past ^:year were the largest made in the history of. the Company. The balance available for appropriation, after making provision fotbad and doubt- ful debts, all repairs to plant and machinery, and other chargesoincluding advertising, was sufficient to allew of the usual final distribution of 44 per cent., and a bonus of half per cent., making a total distribution for the year of 74 per cent. The sum of £5OO was placed to depreciation account, £1,500 to reserve and a balance of £2,157 Os. 11d, carried forward to 1903. The Chairman reminded the shareholders that many people seemed to think the Company only manufactured Sanitas ". Fluid and some few other" Sanitas "pro- prietary articles, but the fact was that they had three factories in this country and in. New York, and were manufacturers on a large scale of all kinds of dis- infectants and many varieties of patent and other sanitary articles, and they had business connections in all parts of the civilised world and particularly in the British Colonies. It was to these circumstances that he attributed at least a part of their continued growth and prosperity, and although competition was terribly keen he was thoroughly satisfied that the Company could well hold its own and might look forward with con- fidence to the future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>PRESENTATION TO MR. THOMAS S. STEEL.</head>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO MR. THOMAS S. STEEL.</p>
        <p>The valuable services rendered to the Warrington District of the Independent Order of Oddfellows (Manchester Unity), during Coronation year by P.P.G.M. Thomas S. Steel were fittingly recognized on Thursday last week when the members of the district assembled at the Pelican Inn, Buttermarket-street, to present Bro. Steel with a gold lever hunting watch. The room was crowded, in fact we are informed that never before has there been such a large attendance at a presentation in connection with the district. This in itself is a tribute to the esteem in which Bro Steel is held by the members. P.G.M. Ellis R. Clare presided and was supported by the prominent district and lodge officers and Brothers David Houghton (Order of Druids) and GeorgeOlaybury (Order of Foresters). The proceedings took the form of a smoking concert, a capital programme being submitted. Songs were ren- dered by P.C. Joseph Taylor and Bros. A Pemberton, R. Suranor, and J. Willett, whilst humorous items were given in excellent style by P.G. E. A Sanders, and Bro. W. W. Whittle. P.P.G. M. Joseph Massey was res- ponsible for a recitation. The loyal toasts were honoured, and also that of "The Manchester Unity and Warrington District" which was proposed by P.G. Wm. Owen of the Cadishead Lodge, and responded to by P. D.G.M. Mr. H. Holford.</p>
        <p>In making the presentation on behalf of the district, P.G. M. Ellis M. Clare spoke of the capable i manner n which P.P.G.M. Steel had performed the duties of his office in his lodge, and mentioned how very quickly he had risen from the position of lodge officer to that of district officer. In Bro. Steel they had had a most capable Grand Master, and no one in the whole of I.:the Warrington district could have been selected to have more ably filled that position during such an important year as Coronation year. The Grand Master also spoke of the difficulties which Brother Steel had to contend with and the anxious time he ex- perienced with reference to the postponement of the Coronation estivities and the death of Prov.C.S. John Emson. He had, however, succeeded in overcoming all difficulties and had performed the duties most credit- ably. In making the presentation he expressed the hope that Bro. Steel might look back with pleasure upon the year he spent as Grand Master of the Warrington Odd- fellows.</p>
        <p>Eulogistic speeches were also delivered by P.P.G.M. Matthew Knowles, P.G. William Holbrook, P.P.G.M. Alban Boyle, P.G. Frederick Taylor (secretary of the Constitution Lodge of which Bro. Steel is a member), and Prov. C.S. F. Webster.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. WALKER, as an oddfellow but not a mem- ber of the Warrington district, paid testimony to the worth of Bro. Steel and referred more particularly to his cordial relations with the local Press. Bros. DAVID HOUGHTON and GEORGE MAYBURY spoke very highly of Bro. Steel's services to Friendly Societies generally, and paid a tribute to his assistance in connection with the Coronation festivities.</p>
        <p>In responding, P.P.G.M. 'TEEL thanked the members very sincerely for their handsome present, and assured them that any service he might have rendered to the district during Coronation year had been given most willingly.. He was deeply interested in Oddfellowship. and was afraid that the remarks made by the various brethren were altogether too flattering. He greatly esteemed their kindness and would always look back with pleasure upon the year in which' he acted as Grand Master for the Warrington Oddfellows. Bro. Steel spoke of several innovations he had introduced during his term of office, and expressed the hope that they would be for the improvement and benefit of the dis- trict generally. It gave him very great pleasure to see that- whatever little service he had rendered to the friendly societies in the town was so much appreciated, as was shown by the attendance of the Chairman and Secretary of the Friendly Societies Council. The watch is a very handsome one and the outside bears Mr. Steel's monogram. Inside the following in- scription appears —" Presented to P.P.G.M. Thomas - S. Steel by the Warrington District of the Independent Order of Oddfellows, (Manchester Unity) for services rendered Coronation year, 1902."</p>
        <p>THE REDUCTION IN WEIGHT OF BICYCLES.--For early two years the Rudge-Whitworth experimental Department and the Chemical and Physical Labor- tones have been establishing data for a complete re design of pattern and re-specification of material for a really light bicycle which should be as strong as or stronger than its predecessors, and be, aboveall, capable of production at a price at which the cycling public can buy it in large quantities, The Aero-Special Rudge- Whitworth is the product of these efforts, and in it is provided for the first time, at a reasonable price, a Roadster Bicycle, fully guaranteed by its makers and up to all ordinary hard' riding, whose weight complete —25 lbs. only—is over 15 per cent. less than last year's Light Roadsters, and whose price is but £l6 16s. Space forbids the description in detail of the many clever inventions and devices that contribute to the perfections of this Bayardamong Cycles. Suffice it isito say that anyone interested can gather all particulars from the admirable Rudge-Whitworth Catalogue, which is sent post-free to all enquiries on application to the Head Office, Coventry, or any of the Depots and Agencies.</p>
