      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR</p>
        <p>-NOTICE TO CORiESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>Authors of letters intended for publication must mall cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Matter intended for publicatison must be written on one site oily of the paper and sent in, if possible,Ft later than Thursday. Correspondents are strongly advised to write briefly. A short letter has a much greater chance of insertion than a long one. We cannot under ordinary circumstances undertake to return the manuscripts which are not used.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>" Cowardice " of Gladstone and Chamberlain.</head>
        <p>The " Cowardice " of Gladstone and Chamberlain.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln reply to the letter of Briton" in last Saturday's issue of your Warrington contemporary, this . writer g says that my story of the negotiations before Majubai is very nice." I have no fancy to be "damned by faint praise,' or weak words. The question is not whether my account of the negotiations is" nice," but whether it is true or false. This correspondence began by my protest to the clearly implied imputation of cowardice to the government of 1881, and necessarily to its chief, Mr. Gladstone, and to his ablest lieutenant Mr. Chamberlain. I showed that the negotiations were begun on the initiation of the Boers, that the basis of peace was laid down before the disaster at Majuba. Mr. Chamberlain in a speech at Birmingham on June 7th, 1881, vigourously defended the ministerial policy. He said that the overtures of peace came, in the first instance, from President Brand, and secondly from Mr. Kruger, the Vice-President of the Boer leaders. The latter wrote to Sir George Colley, to say that he was confident of the justice of his cause and was willinelto submit his case to Royal Commission. On this asis Sir George Colley was ordered by the Government to arrange for a settlement. Now unless Mr. Joynson- Hicksis prepared to add to his former indiscretions, by , charging Mr. Chamberlain with falsehood, as well as I cowardice, then he is bound to acknowledge that the I settlement Mr. Chamberlain speaks of was not the result 1 of cowardice as a result of Majuba, but the completion of a settlement, the basis of which had already been defined. The only way out of the difficulty' for those gentlemen is, either frankly to withdraw the charge of cowardice, (and this would be the wisercourse and ought to have been taken sooner), or to contendi that 3lajuba should have been avenged, and the policy reversed, as a consequence of this disaster. Your readers will await with interest to see which course they propose to adopt. In regard to the acquisition of more territory, I stand by what I have said. Statesmen on both sides are coming to see that the consolidation of the Empire, and not the increase of the mileage of its territory, is the great need of the present time. As Lord Rose- berry told us, we have done quite enough in" staking out claims for posterity". At the beginning of the last century the flag waved over an empire of 5,818,12 square miles, at the end of the century it has risen to 11,6.57,310. square miles. To use an American vulgarism, ewe have bitten off as much as we can chew". What all Englishmen, whatever be their party name, have to realise and belieee with their , whole heart, is that the strength and greht- I ness of the empire is in the men, and not in the mileage. In the physical, intellectual, andmoral health of the sons and daughters. Should the empire become so enfee'oledat its heart on account of its strength be- ing wasted at an extremity, that it cannot meet the calls of wisdom and necessity at home? If statesmen are too timid to adopt the best methods, for lack of resources, of developing the brain power of the youth and nation at home, then it were for us to abandon the principle of grab, which some call our imperial mission and concentrate our energies on the physical, mental and mocal development of our race. I felt sad when I heard the acknowledgement of Mr. Marshall Hall about the Education Bill in Parr Hall. Here was one who frankly, and wisely, acknowledged that the better course would have been to buy up sectarian schools, and establish a thoroughly national system of Education, hut who considered that the carrying out of such a scheme was absolutely barred by the exnaustion of our financial resources. We have spent the savings of 73 years, amounting to 250 millions, in wearing out the i3oers, and this has laid such burdens on the resources of the British people that on the admission of the Unionist politicians we have had to adopt the second best plan, and a very poor second best, in the vital question of education. " Briton " in his indifference to right or wrong in -national affairs is as foolhardy as Mr. Joynson-Hicks. "Briton" says "that men who uphold their country right or wrong "—should command our admiration. I do not think we should be too hard on these gentlemen, I doubt not we should, in private affairs, find that Mr. Joynson-Hicks (and also" Briton" if he were not so bashful about his own identity, of which I daresay he has nothing to be ashamed), apart from national affairs these gentlemen would without question or fiesitancy accept the rule of right as their standard. Any sugges- tion of our* to the contrary would be warmly repudia- ted. When we find them applying another code of ethics, the principle of being top dog right or wrong, to national relationships, and becoming indifferent to stan- dards, which in other spheres of life,"they have learned to honour and obey, we should put it down not so much as a lapse into an immoral state, as an immature development revealing a crudity of perception in regard to the oblig Ilion to the eternal law of right, and its universal application to all the affairs of man. CHARLES E. PARKER. Penketh, February 26th, 1903.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Mr. A. H. Crosfield and Mr. Joynson- Hicks.</head>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield and Mr. Joynson- Hicks.</p>
        <p>Sir,—There is one aspect of the late war and its pre- sent consequences which I have not yet touched in the controversy which Mr. Joynson-Hicks provoked. I mean its bearing on the interests of the working classes in this country and in South Africa. This is a topic which Imperialists of Mr. Joynson-Hicks' school pre- fer to ignore, unless indeed during a general election when circumstances oblige them to remember that whether the working man has special interests or not, he undoubtedly has a vote. Recent events in the Trans- vaal have forcibly reminded me of a very adroit speech which Mr. Chamberlain made at the height of the war fever in 1900. He was addressing the minors of Can- nock Chase, and with the unerring electioneering in- stinct which never fails him, he tried to persuade them that our war with Mr. Kruger was a "Miners' War " a war undertaken on behalf of working men in defence of their interestsa war whose result would be the open- ing up of a vast field for British labourunder theßritish flag. He made a similar appeal to the humanitarian sentiment. We were fighting the Boers for the sake of the natives and the war was, as a great Non-conformist Divine put it, as much a crusade against slavery as the American Civil War. It is a little singular that these two excuses for the war were never mentioned in the negotiations which preceded it. Did Lord Milner remonstrate with Mr. Kruger for oppressing the natives? If so the dispatch have been carefully concealed. Did we demand labour- legislation in the interests of the miners? If so our diplomacy was marvellously secretive in its methods. It is not a little singular that the attitude of Mr. Kniger's government. re-actionary though it was in many ways, was steadily friendly towards labour. The Rand was on the point of passing a Miners' Eight Hours Bill when the war broke out, and Mr. Rose, president of the Rand Labour Union has shown, in his recent book on the war, that President Kruger always received trade union deputations with the utmost courtesy and as a rule adopted their suggestions in any legislation that affected the mines. As for the Franchise we have the confession of Mr. Rhodes's friendsand confidants in 1895, that the Uitlander Democracy "did not care a fig" for it. It was not the oppressed Uitlander but the De Beers Company and the Chartered Company and the Consolidated Goldfields which organised and financed the Jameson Raid. The Cornish miners took the first train for the sea, when the gold magnates raised the flag of revolution. The facts about the natives are no less clear. The Chartered Company under whose flag the mid took place, was at that very moment maintaining in Rhodesia a system which was afterwards described by Sir Richard Martin, the Commissioner sent out by the Colonial Office to -investigate it, as a system of "compulsory labour" which to the natives must have seemed nothing less than slavery itself." What the demands of the Rhodes group were in the Transvaal itself, we have from the report of the Indus- trial Commission appointed by Mr. Kruger to inquire into the grievances of the Mine Owners shortly after the Raid. One after another these Mine Owners demanded the imposition of a tax to drive the natives into the mines, and they also suggested that the Boer Government should use its police to recruit labour for the mines. Should imitate, in other words, the system -which the Chartered Company had established in Rhodesia. I quote one brief passage from the evidence of a wealthy patriot who bears the good old Anglo- , Saxon name of Albu :</p>
        <p>"The native at the present time receives a wage which is far in excess of the exigencies of his exist- ence I think if the native gets enough pay to save .£5 a year, that sum is quite enough for his requirements, and will prevent natives from becoming rich in a short space of time." Asked whether it was in the power of the mining indus- try to regulate the wages of the Kaffirs, Mr. Albu replies : "To a great extent it is, provided that Government assist us in bringing labour to the market." There is the policy fairly stated. The Rand financiers wanted to reduce the current rate of wages, but in order to effect this it was necessary that they should have a government which would force the natives to work in the mines. It was the redeeming feature of Mr. Kruger's native policy that he refused to make sweating possible by violence, but that refusal cost him dear. The I mine owners knew how to exploit the rucial feeling in South Africa, and these aliens who could hardly repeat "Rule Brittania " in English were astute enough to maintain newspapers in every South African town to beat the patriotic drum at the bidding of the Album, the: Belts and the Ecksteins. Their motive for all this, as 161 r. Rhodes said, was "practical business." They had</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Examiner, Friday, March 6, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Friday, March 6, 1903.</p>
        <p>ed Piokavanco, jun., and W. Nightingale were appoint representatives upon the Infirmary Board of Manag ment.</p>
        <p>THE COMMITTEE'S REPRESENTATION ON THE EDUCATION AUTHORITY.</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY reported the receipt of a letter from. the Town Clerk asking the committee to nominate a representative to be co-opted on the new education authority as soon as possible. The MAvoa said it must be a source of satisfaction and pride to the committee to have a representative on the New Education CoMmittee. • It showed that the Town Council fully appreciated the representative character of this body and the good work which it was doing, and recognized that education consisted not only in book learning and mansal and technical skill, but also in the great principals of philanthropy and its many sided attributes and accompaniments, including the scientific inventions and curative treatments,where- by the ills to which humanity was subject might be relieved and remedied. It now rested with the com- mittee to take the important step of nominating a gentleman to serve them on the Education Committee. Mr. NIGHTINGALE made a long speech in regard to educational matters in general and expressed the hope that the committee would appoint a representative who would do them credit. They held a great responsibility to the children, who should be given an education whereby they would have a chance of holding their own with Germany and America, who, we were told, were outstripping this country. He was astounded that an intelligent borough like_ Warrington should have been so long behind in the race of education. He was thank- ful to the Town Council, though he was not on that body yet—(laughter)—for giving that committee what in honour they had a right to.</p>
        <p>--Mr. Pori moved that Mr. Nightingale should be the nomin?e, and :Inspector Taylor seconded. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Go. WALkEft moved as an amendment that Mr. Joseph Taylor be their representative. Mr. WATK IN seconded.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr. WADSWORTIL, secondel by Mr. SMITH, the matter was postponed to a speDial meet- ing to be held in about a fortnight, the first proposition and amendment being withdrawn.</p>
        <p>VOTES OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>ALTERATION IN THE INFIRMARY RULES.</p>
        <p>A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the Chair- man, Deputy Chairman, and Secretary for their services. In moving a vote of thanks to the members of the Warrington Cycling Club and all who assisted in the Fancy Dress Parade and Carnival, Mr. F. MONKS spoke of the gratifying result of that event, which, he said, was unique and ,showed an amount of invention and ingenuity which many of them did not expect. He took that opportunity to refer to the alterations in the rules of the Infirmary. At their meeting on Friday, rule 91 about which there had been considerable discussion WAS altered. It was not quite what the Workingmen's Com- mittee passed, but practically carried out the same idea. It now read person shall be admitted as an out- patient who is in receipt of parochial relief, or whose weekly income exceeds the following scale :—One person in the family. 17s, per week; two persons in the family, 21s. per week; three persons in the family, 255. per week ; and 3s. per week for each additional member of the family. When a special recom- mendation is given to any patient by a works manager or representative of the Workingmen's Infirmary Committee, it shall promptly receive the necessary attention at the Infirmary. If the House Surgeon is aware of any reason for objecting to such recommendation.he shall instruct the Secretary to in- vestigate the case and report thereon to the House Committee, whose decision shall be communicated without delay to the giver of the recommendation." The reasons for the objection would be recorded on the House Committee's minute book and members of the Workingmen's Committee would have the opportunity of asking questions when they came up for confirmation. What the Workingmen's Committee recommended in re- gard to the amounts was 18s., 215., and 245. respectively, but the Board of Management had made what they con- sidered to be a more favourable scale. Alderman MONKS seconded awl the motion was carried.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOHN FSIRCLOUGH proposed a vote of thanks to the Chief Constable and police, the Warrington Rugby Football Club, and the Committee of May-day pro- cession of horses for their efforts to raise funds for the institution and Councillor BURTON seconded. The resolntion was carried._</p>
        <p>Mr. GEO. THOMPSON moved a vote of thanks to the hon. medical officers, senior and junior house surgeons, and mitroa and nurses.. He said it was gratifying that the medical gentlemen in the town had taken up the question with regard to crippled children. At Chester a ladies' committee collected about £lOO a year and sent the children to the seaside, and he thought it would be worth while a ladies committee in Warrington taking up the matter if they cluld be induced to do so. Mr. NIGHINGALE seconded, and the motion was passed.</p>
      </div>
