        <p>the City on th; 11th November, 1899 :7</p>
        <p>- - "They should try some cogent forni of induce- ment or practically compel the native, through taxation or in some other way, to contribute his quota to the good of the community. . . . We have in power to-day a strong Government, but there is a morbid sentimentality among a large sec- tion of the community on the subject of the natives If kfr. Rhodes and Sir Alfred Milner could put their heads together the effect would be that they would be able to propose some- thing practical in this direction." The expectation that Lord Milner would support the policy of forced labour which Mr. Kruger had opposed, was perhaps the most powerful of all the motives which induced the gold industry to provoke the late war. The war was not concluded before the Gold Trust realized the first item of its programme. It reduced the wages of natives by practically 50 per cent. The inevitable happened. There was a great scarcity of labour. The demand in other directions was brisk— for railway construction, for the re-building of burned</p>
