ve of workingmen and not by the office.. He was not going to enter into a wordy warfare in regard to what had been said in the Press as to the codlings of the Trades Council. He had nothing to say against them for the work they were doing, but he thought they might have left out any attack upon that committee. The Workingmen's Committee were legitimately elected by the employs of the various ' works, and be considered they ' had a claim to any honours that the Town Council or anyone else might think fit to grant to a body of working men like they I were. They represented the whole of the working men in Warrington, and not a certain few. (Applause.) REPRESENTATIVES ON THE BOARD OF MANAGEMENT.</p>
