      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>SEAWEED AS A MEDICINE.</head>
        <p>SEAWEED AS A MEDICINE.</p>
        <p>ITS MARVELLOUS EFFECT UPON THE</p>
        <p>STOMACH, LIVER, KIDNEYS AND BOWELS. SEAWEED possesses a natural strengthening, healing and purifying power, far greater than other known remedies. This is now conceded by important authori- ties. It was first introduced by Mr. Veno. and Venn's Seaweed Tonic is the only remedy in the world contain- ing seaweed, for that reason Veno's Seaweed Tonic is used in hospitals and by doctors themselves because of its superior curative properties. Its most brilliant effect is produced in stomach, liver and kidney diseases, and the extraordinary cures it performs, even in the worst cases, is positive proof of its efficacy. It is a god-send to sufferers from indigestion, wind, headache, general weakness, kidney trouble, weak and painful back, torpid liver, female troubles, poorness of blood, and HABITUAL CONSTIPATION. Ask for VENO'S SEA- WEED TONIC. Prices Is. 11,41. and 2s. 9d. at chemists and medicine vendors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>LATE MR. WILLIAM GLEAVE.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. WILLIAM GLEAVE.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. THOMP4ON said ke had been grieve to hear of the death of Mr. William Gleave. He was a member of the committee when he (Mr. Thompson) joined it and was willing to do everything he possibly could for the benefit of the institution. Re moved a vote of con- dolence with the family the late Mr. Gleave iii their bereavement. -</p>
        <p>Mr. F. MONKS seconded and spoke of Mr. Gleave's valuable services. He had known him for nearly forty years and could only confirm what Mr. Thompson had said.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOSEPH TAYLOR supported and said Mr. Gleave was always present at any meetings of the General or Executive Committee of which he received notice. The motion was carried. THE MAXOR THNNKED.</p>
        <p>Councillor JoHN ROBERTS proposed a vote of thanks to till Mayor for presiding and for the use of the Council Chamber. He remarked that the Mayor was always ready and willing to help a good cause. (Applause.) Councillor BURTON seconded, and the motion was passed with acclamation. In reply the MAYOR said he felt it an honour and a great pleasure to occupy the position of Chairman of the m3eting. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>GREAT GALE.</head>
        <p>THE GREAT GALE.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn.</p>
        <p>On Thursday night a gale of unprecedented violence swept over Runcorn and neighbourhood. A good deal of damage was done to property by the violence of the wind, chimney pots and stacks being blown down in many cases falling through the roof. At "Belle Vue," the residence of Mr. J. H. Frith, and "Lyndhurst," the rasidence of Mr. F. J. Norman the stacks were blown down, crashing through the roof in the former case. Several of the bill-posting stations were wrecked, the stalls at the market were smashed and a portion of the football field hoarding Wa.9 blown down. On Thursday night the steam tug St. Winifred was blown ashore at Ditton, and the Earl of Ellesmere was occupied for over four hours in towing four flats from Weston Point to the Runcorn Bridgewater Lock. On Friday morning the tide was up two hours before time and the river was exceptionally rough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>At Eariestown.</head>
        <p>At Eariestown.</p>
        <p>The storm which raged during the early hours of Friday morning did considerable damage in Earlestown and Newton, but fortunately there was no injury to the inhabitants. Hoardings were blown down in various parts of the district, and. slates and ridging tiles in- numerable were thrown into the streets. A chimney stack was swept off the house occupied by Mr.o,,vbridge in Wargrave-road, and the roofs of one or two houses in Crow-lane were completely stripped and laid bare. The residence of Dr. Mouncey, which has only recently been built was minus many of the roof tiles when morning dawned, and a number of boards were carried from the timber stocks in the yard of Mr. Bunting; and dropped some distance away. In Ashton-road one or two trees were blown down and across the new South Lancashire tramway. Other minor damages consisted of windows blown in bat considering the exposed nature of the dis- trict the damage cannot be said to be extensive.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>LANC.iSHIRE JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD RANGERS V. WEST LEIGH_CATHOLICS.-</p>
        <p>These teams Met on the ground of the Rangers on Saturday. The opening phases of the match were of a give-and-take nature, and it was not until near the in- terval that the Rangers asserted themselves and superior combination led up to Brown scoring a clever I try. This was the only tangible record of the match, for although the Rangers again attacked vigorously I during the second moiety the defence was very sound I and at the conclusion the score rend as follows ;- 1 Latchford Rangers, one try (three points) ; West Leigh I Catholics, nil.</p>
        <p>Returning from India. The South Lancashire Regiment which is also known as "The Prince of Wales's Volunteers," will soon be represented at home by the second battalion (formerly the 82nd Foot), which is ,now on its way home from India. The • battalion contains in its ranks many Liverpool men. Its depot is at Orford Barracks. Colonel Richardson, now commanding the Bth and 40th Regimental Districts, only comparatively re- cently relinquished the command of the battalion, which, on return home, is to he stationed. at Dover. FIRE GRA'tES of every description.—J. S. GAR- LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street,Warringion.—Advt.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Foit CLASS AND CTIINA. of every description try J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. A GOOD BESOLUTION.—WeHr Ara' TAYLOR'S Wonderful Boots.—Advt. PUBLIC BENHFIT, UNDER the CLOCK for BEST BOOTS in LANCASIIIRE.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>Another victory fell to the Warrington Club on Saturday, when Hull were defeated at Wilderspool by seven points to three. The margin is only small and does not indicate much superiority, but it sufficed to gain two points and that was the principal thing. Points were badly needed, and with the result of Saturday's match the fear of the Second Divisioa boge3- has evaporated:</p>
        <p>The Hull men did not relish or expbct a defeat. They have been playing well of late, and this good, form compared with Warrington's inferior achieve- ments made them very hopeful: Warrington appear to be itnproving,however,and with an almost full team in the field they gave a smart display against the "all blacks." The whole of the points in the match were obtained before half time, Dickenson scoring a goal and a try and Isherwood a goal. Parry saved the sea- porters from being nilled by getting a try at the corner after smart combination. The game was an excellent one to watch, both teams playing open foot- ball and relying on speed and cleverness for points. Hull are one of the few Yorkshire teams which do play this kind of football, and they are all the more wel- come on that account. They almost deserved to have drawn the match, so well did they play, but Warrington were just a bit too good for them in one or two respects.</p>
        <p>I remarked last week that it • generally froze or rained when Warrington had a home match. On Saturday morning however, the elements set about to disprove the statement and brilliant sunshine prevailed. One almost regretted the aspersion, when there was suddenly a change, and by the time for kicking off the rain was falling steadily in the old sweet way. Con- sequently the gate suffered,but under the circumstances :£6O was not bad.</p>
        <p>The battle of the "giants" was one of the features of the match. Franks on the Hull side, and Edmunds for Warrington, waged a deadly duel. They were well matched in regard to height, and neither would probably touch the five feet mark, while there was very little disparity in their respective .trickiness. Franks was, perhaps, the better of the two ; he get away from the.i scrummage very cleverly but there was very little m t. The two together would make a very smart pair but as Warringt m won't part with Edmunds and .Hull mean to stick to 'Franks they are not likely to ,have the opportunity of performing on the same side. Un- fortunately for Warrington Edmunds was injured ten minutes from the close and had to be carried off. My readers will be glad to learn, however, that the popular " Tich " is alright once more and would have played to-day, but for the fact that the committee decided he had better rest.</p>
        <p>Warrington's chief advantage on Saturday was un- questionably at centre-threequarters, where Isherwood and Dickenson 'played fine football.. The two together were responsible for the whole of Warrington's points, and very largely for the fact that Hull were kept from sco ing again. Their work was worthy of high praise. In other departments Hull were, if anything, rather superior. On the wing they had two better and speldier men than 'Harris and Hockenhall, while Taylor, at full back, and the forwards claimed a slight advantage. With the Warrington centres, the Hull men would probably have won by a good margin.</p>
        <p>OA the hour) side praise is also due to Duckworth, who played as well as he did in those glorious days when Warrington ran into the final for the cup. He and Edmunds make the most effective pair Warrington have played behind the " scrum" this season. The forwards again improved and (rave a good account of ' themselves even when one short. Fish was badly missed, in fact the scoring power of the wings was too conspicuously absent to be gratifying. With Isherwood in such fine form Fish would Probably have pierced the Hull defence.</p>
        <p>There has been some more chopping and changing in the League table. The most sensational perform- ance of last Saturday was undoubtedly that of St. Helens, the wooden-spoonists,- who actaally defeated Halifax, the leaders--,-and by .superior football too. The weather of course, suited the Saints, but no one expected them to overthrow the Hanson-lane brigade. Swinton did well•to draw at Batley, while Broughton Just managed to " pip " the burly Bradfordians.</p>
        <p>Runcorn deserve a special" par " to themselves. On Saturday they took down 'Salford by 2 goals to nil, and on Wednesday they entertained• Broughton with equal warmth, the score being 9-3 itt favour of the Linnets. To lower the colours of the three Man- chester clubs in successive matches marks Runcorn as very strong candidates for premier honours. • They have a chance for three cups now, and I wish them well in the struggle —for two of them. They might leave the West Lancashire trophy for poor War- rington !</p>
        <p>Warrington have now got six clubs :below them, and with 22 points out of 21m-itclies are not doing so badly. After all I believe they will attain the sun- mit of their ambition once more by reaching the centre of the table.</p>
        <p>The Levu? table up to date is as follows : For Agst.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. 1). G. T. P. G.T. P. I'. Halifax 26...17_ 7_2...23 29-133...15 15— 75...36 Salford 26...15... 7...4...21 40-168_19 16— 86-34 *Swinton 25_13_ 5...7...31 44-194...17 17— 85...33 Runcorn 21_14... 9...4...27 39-171...15 14— 72..32 Bro'ght'n R 26_13_ 8...5...23 39-163...16 16— 80...31 *Oldham 23...14... 9...0...20 36-148...16 15— 77...28 Bradford 25_12_10...3_26 35157...21 24-114...27 Bstley 25...12...10...3...27 27-135...26 25-127...27 Hunslet 25...12_11_2_36 21-135...28 40-176...26 Hull 26...12...12...2...15 33-129...23 27-127...26 Leigh 25...10...10...5...24 18-102...18 2A-108-25 Warrington 24... 9...11...L.16 18— 86...22 28-128...22 Wigan 23... 8...11...4...20 18— 94...20 22-106...20 Widnes 25... 9...15...1...19 17— 89...19 30-128...19 Huddersfield 25... 8...15...2...10 21— 83...27 32-150...18 Hull I. R. 24... 8...14...2...11 79...29 36-166_18 Brighouse R 26... 6_17...3_15 11— 63...28 36-164...15 St Helens 25.......19...1...22 11— 77...30 59-237...11 The ':'.•;winton v. Oldham match has been ordered to be repilyed, and has been struck out of the above table.</p>
        <p>The cup-tie are resumed to-day, and the follpwing matches in the third round will take Place : Milton] v. Hull. Rochdale Hornets v. Salford. Runcorn v. Maryport. Hunslet v. Leer Ls. Bradford v. Oldham. Halifax v. Brighouse Rangers. Barrow v. Batley. Keighley v.. York.</p>
        <p>In consequence of Oldham playing at Bradford, Warrim.,rt.n have no engagement. The "A" team will entertain Oldham " " however, and the follow- ing warm fifteen has been selected to meet the Water- shedding.; contingent : Knight, full back; Fish Brooks, Bailey and Watkins, three-quarters; Burgess and Wilkinson, half-backs; Boardman, Morrison, Creswell, Warburton, Coleman, Harmer, Gallop and Lunt, forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Foot-Racing.</head>
        <p>Foot-Racing.</p>
        <p>A committee meeting of the National Cross- country Union was held in Manchester on Saturday evening, when the teams sent in to represent the fol- lowing clubs in the National Championship at Hay- dock Park to-day (S.itarday) were passed en bloc : Highgate, Essex Beagles, Salford, Birclffield, Crewe, Small Heath, South London, Cheshire Tally Ho, Fara- worth (Widnes), Sankey, Sefton, Sutton, Leeds A.C., Manchester, and Warrington. The officials for the championship were elided, and it was decided that twelve runners form the team to represent England in the International race at Glasgow on March 28th. The respective Northern, Midland, and Southern champi9llS—J. Hosker (Farnworth),G. Smith (Derby), and A Shrubb (South London) were chosen, and the remainingnine will be selected after the decision of the National Championship to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>Angling,</head>
        <p>Angling,</p>
        <p>It is reported that the close time for coarse fish March 15th to June 15th) is to be applied to the (Bridgewater Canal throughout its entire length. At the meeting of the Travellers' Rest Angling Society on Monday evening last, the cup, which has been pre- sented to the club for a competition for bream, was on view. The Perseverance Angling Society have decided to have no special " sett " prizes, but to go in more for matches. Several members have, however, offered prizes for specimen dace, gudgeon and bream. The Mersey A.S. have decided to have an opening match on June 21st at Vale Royal. The Latchford Excelsior A.S. have fixed the dates of their matches</p>
        <p>as follows 26th and September 13th. Very few local anglers ventured out last week end. Mr. William Hamblett did well at Llangollen on Monday last, two dace of 10ozs. sdrs. and Bozs. Bdrs. respec- tively, and two roach of about a pound each were his best fish. Angling prospects for this week-end are i mproved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-03-06_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>RUNCORN BOATING CLUB. Annual General Meeting.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOATING CLUB. Annual General Meeting.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening, at 8 o'clock, the 9th annual general meeting of the members of the Runcorn Boat- ing Club was held in the Brunner Guild Hall, there be- ing a very goodattendance. Mr. Bennion was voted to the chair,and in his opening remarks said he was pleased to see such a good attendance. It would be a good thing if they could get together in such a concrete bodymore frequently. It gave a real zest to the business of the club generally, and he hoped that, in the future, the club would launch out and participate in the regattas which were held, such as Chester &amp;c. and perhaps by doing so the interest of the people of Runcorn would be aroused to the pitch it attained in the days of old at the time of the Runcorn Gap. He also urged the members to do their utmost to extend the list of membership as they were now inlpossession of a splendid fleet of boats in a position to supply the wants of an increased Roll of Membership. He would also like to call the attention of the Members to the New Secretary, Mr. Hulse. Having been a Member of the Committee for a number of years, and knowing to a certain extent the immensity of the work connected with the office of Secretary, he would say that, although they had had good energetic gentlemen at the head of affairs in the past, yet the Boating Club had never be fore been guided by such on able,enterprising and persevering Secretary as Mr. Hulse had proved himself to be by his accomplishments during the past season. The first business on the Agenda was the Secre- tary's( Mr. Albert E. Hulse's) Report,which was aslfollows: —lt affords me great pleasure to be able to present to youott this our Ninth Annual General Meeting, a summary of the past season's work, which I am sure you will readily acknowledge is one denoting progress info smell way. During the past year, 25 members have been admitted to the privileges of the Club, and, taking into account the withdrawals, the numbers stand much the same as for the preceding year. In the report for last year it was stated that plans and specifications had been prepared for the erection of a new boat house which would also provide more &amp;Relent dressing room accommodation. Ati the beginning of the season,tenders were invited for the execution of the workand the com- mittee accepted Messrs. Farrell's. This decision of your committee was protested against a..d a special general meeting was held onlApri I :lath when, after due considera- tion, you agreed with the decision of your committee. Immediately following this, Mr. F. Abel and W. A. Millinger, captain and secretary respectively, deemed it necessary to resign and a special general meeting was held on the 13th May for the purpose of appointing their successors. Mr. J. W. Wiswall (then sub-captain) was appointed captain, Mr. J. Savage, sub-captain, and myself, secretary. The work of erecting the new boat house was then commenced by Messrs earrell,and under the able superintendence of Mr. J. W. Wiswall, the work was completed in the specified time, and thus we materially increased the value of the club's property by , She addition of a substantial and convenient beat house. Or thanks are due to Mr. IWiswall for his kindness in preparing plans : and specifications and for superintiending the work. I then pass on to what I term the most successful of the undertakings of your Committee during e the past season, viz :—rhe gala. Never before had such a gathering been brought together on the banks of the Weaver, and, under splendid atmospheric conditions, the second annual regatta and swimming gala took place on Bank Holiday, August 4th. To estimate the interest taken in the event, it was only necessary to see the eleven hundred people present. It was a success both from a spectacular and financial point of view. The entries, both for the boating and swintming races, were good, and a splendid afternoon's sport was provided, and, I feel sure, appre- ciated. Probably the greatest interest was aroused in the Quarter Mile Skiff Race for the club championship and silver cup. Mr. W. A. Millinger, the holder of the cup, having won it on two successive occasions, again ' proved himself the champion of the club, and thus the Cup becamehis own permanent property. It was very I pleasing to notice the great interest taken in the boat races by the members, this being evidenced by the great I amount of training undertaken by them before the Regatta. The financial result, however, was equally satisfactory. The accounts -showed an income of £6O 18s. 3d., expenditure £39 19s. 5d., leaving a profit to ' the funds of the club of £2O 18s. 10d. Your Committee are very pleased to be able to record such a success, and that the club funds benefitted so extensively. There can be no doubt but that the event will be one of the most popular annuals in the district if you will only exhibit in the future that lively enthusiasm which you so willingly displayed at. the last gala, by helping the committee to the best of your ability to make the gala more successful each year. Your committee de- cided to hold a picnic on August 31st to Acton Bridge I and a most enjoyable outing was experienced. The only regrettable feature was the scarcity of members. Referring to the matter of dressing room accommoda- tion, I may say that a scheme has already been pre- pared for the equipment of the dressing room and will be laid before the incoming committee at the earliest opportunity. One boat has been purchased, a two-pair scull, providing accommodation for two persons. No accidents of any description have to be recorded this year. I would like to express the thanks of the club to our caretaker, Mr. J. Thomas, for the effieient manner in which he has looked after the Club's property during the past season. Yours obediently, ALBERT E. HuLsE, Honorary Secretary. On the motion of Mr. .1. W. Wiswall, seconded by Mr. W. Booth, the Secretary's. Report was adopted, and a hearty vote of thanks accorded him for his services during the past season. Mr. H. G. Johnson, Treasurer, then presented his Annual Report regarding the Club's Financial position. 1902 had been the heaviest year the Club had had to face since its inauguration. The accounts showed an income of £lOO 2s 9d. expenditure £9B 3s 3d. leaving a balance to be carried forward of £1 19s 6d. This was. very satisfactory indeed but he would like to mention that one or two 'accounts were still outstanding and would be met at a very early date. He was very pleased to have such a statement, to place before them taking- into account the very heavy expenditure in con- nection with the erection of the New Boathouse. On the motion of Mr. H. McNorton, seconded by Mr. H. Jones, the Treasurer's report was adopted and the thanks of members tendered him for his work during the past season. It was decided to alter rule 4 so as to provide for an entrance fee of 2s. Gd. and a new rule was passed pre- venting the carrying of firearms in the club's boats. I It was proposed by the Secretary seconded by Mr. Beck and carried that the following gentlemen be elected members of the club :—Messrs. Groome, H. Uns- worth, F. Jones, S. Ennion,and L. Dyer. A vote of thanks was accorded to the outgoing com- mittee on the proposition of Mr.H.McNorton seconded by the Secretary.</p>
        <p>The election of officers resulted as follows :—Presi- dent, Sir R. M. Brooke Bart., vice-presidents, Sir .1. T. Brunon Bart. and Messrs. C. N. Trven. H. Baker, T. F. Hazlehurst. j. Taylor, J.P., and Colonel Hall Walker ; secretary, Mr. Albert E. Hulse; treasurer, Vr. H. G. Johnson; captain, Mr. Jr. W. ; sub-captain, Mr. Robert Johnson; committee, Messrs. R. Bennion, W. .T. Beck, I'. G. Moore, H. Jones, J. G. Milling- ton, R. P. :Johnson and J. Peacock.</p>
        <p>X vote of thanks to the Chairman brought the meet- ing to a termination.</p>
        <p>London and North-Western Railway Bill. The London and North-Westcrn Railway Bill was before the examiners of standing orders in the Ilonie of Lords on Thursday. The company proposes to acquire property in Nant wieh and Warrington, in Chesil ire,and at Garston, Newton-in-Makerfield,Burton, Broughton, Morecambe, and Carnforth in Lancashire for the purpose of tasking extensions of the line. The examiner fund that the further standing orders ap- plieable had been complied with, and ordered the measure to be reported to the House for second reading.</p>
